/* =====================================================
   FUGA MYTHICA — ROOT_STORIES: origin stories for Cursus root families
   shared: Latin/Greek root ids used by en/fr/es/it  ·  de: German stem ids  ·  la: Latin pack ids
   laMap / deMap: fall back from a Latin / German id to a shared story
   value = [zhStory, enStory]  — 讲来历、历史用法、进入今天语言的路径;不罗列衍生词(卡片另有同根词栏)
   ===================================================== */
const ROOT_STORIES = { shared: {}, de: {}, la: {}, laMap: {}, deMap: {}, alias: {} };
Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.shared, {
  ven: ["印欧语根 *gʷem-(走、来)是欧洲最古老的动词之一:它在英语里变成 come,在希腊语里变成 bainō(走),在拉丁语里变成 venio。罗马人把\"来\"用在一切场合:军团 ad-venit(到达),元老们 con-veniunt(聚在一起,后来的 convention),真理 in-venitur(被\"走到\"、被发现,inventio 本义是找到而非发明)。1066 年诺曼人把这套词带进英语,于是 advent、event、invent、prevent、revenue(re-venue,\"回来的钱\")都是同一个动作\"来\"的不同方向。",
    "PIE *gʷem- (to go, come) is one of Europe's oldest verbs: it became English come, Greek bainō, Latin venio. Romans used \"come\" for everything: a legion ad-venit (arrives), senators con-veniunt (come together — the later convention), truth in-venitur (is \"come upon\", found: inventio meant discovery, not invention). The Normans carried the set into English after 1066, so advent, event, invent, prevent and revenue (\"money that comes back\") are all the one act of coming, pointed in different directions."],
  leg: ["拉丁语 legere 最初是农夫的动作:\"捡、拾\"——捡柴、拾穗(colligere,收集)。从捡东西到\"挑选\"(eligere,选出;后来的 election),再到\"用眼睛一个个捡起字母\"——这就是\"阅读\"。罗马人读书是出声读,lectio 是\"读一段\",lector 是读给主人听的奴隶。同一个根在希腊语里是 legein(说、算),所以 logos(话语、道理)、-logy(学问)是它的希腊表亲。西塞罗说 religio 也来自 relegere(反复拾取、恭敬地重温)——一个词根,从麦田走到了神庙。",
    "Latin legere began as a farmer's gesture: to pick up, gather — firewood, ears of grain (colligere, to collect). From picking things to picking out (eligere, election), then to picking up letters one by one with the eye: reading. Romans read aloud; a lectio was a reading, a lector the slave who read to his master. The same root in Greek is legein (to say, count), so logos and -logy are its Greek cousins. Cicero derived religio from relegere, to go over again with care — one root walking from the wheat field to the temple."],
  vert: ["vertere(转)来自印欧语根 *wert-,同源的英语词是 -ward(toward,朝向)和 worth(古义\"变成\")。罗马农夫用它说犁地翻土(vertere terram),诗人用它说命运之轮翻转,译者用它说把希腊文\"转\"成拉丁文(vertere 也是\"翻译\")。versus 本是\"犁到田头转回来的一行\",后来指诗的一行——诗行就是一次转身。公元 4 世纪基督徒把 conversio 用作\"皈依\":把整个人生转个方向。今天 version、divorce(转开)、universe(转成一体)、adverse(转来对着你)都还在转。",
    "vertere (to turn) comes from PIE *wert-; its English cousins are -ward (toward) and worth (originally \"to become\"). Roman farmers turned the soil with it (vertere terram), poets turned Fortune's wheel, translators \"turned\" Greek into Latin (vertere also meant to translate). A versus was first the furrow where the plough turned back at the field's edge, then a line of poetry — every verse is a turning. Fourth-century Christians made conversio mean turning one's whole life around. Version, divorce (a turning away), universe (turned into one) and adverse (turned against you) are still turning."],
  cap: ["capere(抓、拿)与英语 have、德语 haben 同源(印欧语 *kap-,日耳曼语里 k 变成 h)。它是罗马法律与战争的核心动词:captivus 是战场上被\"抓住\"的俘虏,capere 也指\"占领城市\"。前缀一加,意思就分岔:ac-cipere 接受、con-cipere 怀孕/构想(把种子\"抓在一起\")、de-cipere 骗(把人\"抓下去\")、ex-cipere 例外(从中拿出)、per-cipere 感知(彻底抓住)。中世纪法语把词尾磨成 -ceive、-ceipt,于是英语有了 receive、deceive、receipt——都是抓的姿势。",
    "capere (to seize, take) shares PIE *kap- with English have and German haben (Germanic turned k into h). It was the key verb of Roman law and war: a captivus was one seized on the battlefield, and capere also meant to take a city. Prefixes split the meaning: ac-cipere to accept, con-cipere to conceive (seize seed together), de-cipere to deceive (take someone down), ex-cipere to except (take out), per-cipere to perceive (grasp thoroughly). Medieval French wore the endings down to -ceive and -ceipt, giving English receive, deceive, receipt — all still gestures of grasping."],
  sta: ["*steh₂-(站立)是印欧语最稳固的词根,英语 stand、德语 stehen、希腊语 histēmi、拉丁语 stare 全是它。罗马人把\"站\"变成了制度:status 是你\"站\"在社会的哪个位置(身份),statua 是站着不动的像,statio 是哨兵站岗的地方(后来的 station)。constare 本义\"站在一起、稳固\",于是 constant、cost(constare 也指\"价格定在多少\")。stare 的分词 status 在中世纪拉丁语变成 \"国家\"(status rei publicae,公共事务的状态)——state、estate、Etat 都从这里来。",
    "*steh₂- (to stand) is the steadiest root in Indo-European: English stand, German stehen, Greek histēmi, Latin stare. Rome turned standing into institutions: status is where you stand in society, a statua stands still, a statio is where the sentry stands (later station). constare, to stand together firmly, gave constant and cost (constare also meant \"to be priced at\"). In medieval Latin the participle status came to mean the condition of public affairs — status rei publicae — and from there state, estate and the French État."],
  duc: ["ducere(引导、牵)本义很具体:牵牛、引水。罗马人用 aquae ductus 把山泉引进城里(高架水道),用 dux 称呼把军队\"牵\"向战场的将领——这个词后来成了公爵 duke、意大利的 Duce。e-ducere 是\"把孩子从里面引出来\"(教育 education 的本义是引出而非灌入),intro-ducere 引进,pro-ducere 引向前(生产),se-ducere 引到一边(诱惑)。中世纪法语磨掉了 c,英语才有 duke、conduit(水管)和 -duce 这一整套。",
    "ducere (to lead, draw) was concrete: leading an ox, drawing water. Romans drew mountain springs into the city through an aquae ductus (aqueduct) and called the man who led the army a dux — later duke and Italy's Duce. e-ducere means to lead the child out (education is drawing out, not pouring in), intro-ducere to lead inside, pro-ducere to lead forward (to produce), se-ducere to lead aside (to seduce). Medieval French dropped the c, giving English duke, conduit and the whole -duce family."],
  ced: ["cedere(走、让步)是罗马政治的动词。元老院辩论时,议员起身\"走\"到自己赞同的一方(in sententiam cedere),这是投票;败方 con-cedit(让步)。公元前 494 年平民集体\"走出\"罗马、退到圣山,史称 secessio plebis——secede 的第一次。pro-cedere 向前走(procedure 是走的步骤),suc-cedere 从下面接着走上来(继承者 successor,顺利跟上就是 success),ex-cedere 走过头(excess),ne-cesse 是\"不可退让\"(necessary)。",
    "cedere (to go, to yield) was the verb of Roman politics. In senate debates a member rose and walked over to the side he supported (in sententiam cedere) — that was the vote; the losers con-cedunt (concede). In 494 BC the plebeians walked out of Rome to the Sacred Mount, the secessio plebis — the first secession. pro-cedere is to go forward (a procedure is the steps taken), suc-cedere to come up from below and follow on (successor; keeping up is success), ex-cedere to go too far (excess), and ne-cesse is what cannot be yielded — necessary."],
  tend: ["tendere(伸展、拉紧)来自印欧语 *ten-(拉),同根有希腊语 tonos(拉紧的弦,tone)和英语 thin(拉薄)。罗马士兵 tendere 帐篷(tentorium,tent),弓箭手 tendere 弓,演说家把注意力\"伸向\"听众——at-tendere(attend、attention)。in-tendere 是把弓拉向目标(intend,意图),con-tendere 是拉到互相较劲(contend),ex-tendere 伸长(extend),por-tendere 伸到前面预示(portent)。os-tendere 是\"伸到人眼前\"——展示;而 pre-tend 本义\"伸在前面当幌子\",假装。",
    "tendere (to stretch, strain) comes from PIE *ten-, which also gave Greek tonos (a stretched string, tone) and English thin (stretched out). Roman soldiers stretched a tent (tentorium), archers stretched the bow, and orators stretched their listeners' minds toward them — at-tendere, attention. in-tendere is drawing the bow at a target (to intend), con-tendere straining against each other (contend), ex-tendere stretching out, por-tendere stretching forward as an omen (portent). os-tendere puts something before your eyes — to show; pre-tend originally means to stretch something in front as a screen."],
  port: ["portare(搬运)与 portus(港口)、porta(城门)是一家:印欧语 *per-(穿过、渡过)。港口是货物\"渡过\"海的地方,城门是人\"穿过\"城墙的地方,portare 就是把东西从这头运到那头。罗马的搬运工叫 portitor,收关税的也叫 portitor——因为货过港口就要缴税。im-portare、ex-portare 是货物进出港口;re-portare 是把消息\"运回来\"(report);sup-portare 从下面托住(support);trans-portare 运过去。opportunus 原指\"顺风把船吹向港口\"——机会 opportunity 就是顺风。",
    "portare (to carry) belongs with portus (harbour) and porta (city gate): PIE *per-, to cross over. A harbour is where goods cross the sea, a gate where people pass through the wall, portare is moving things from here to there. Rome's porters were portitores — and so were its customs men, since goods paid duty as they came through the port. im-portare and ex-portare move goods in and out of harbour; re-portare carries news back (report); sup-portare carries from underneath (support). opportunus meant a wind blowing the ship toward port — opportunity is a fair wind."],
  vid: ["videre(看)来自印欧语 *weid-(看见,因而知道),同根词有希腊语 idea(看到的形象)、历史 historia(希腊 histōr,\"看过的人\"、证人)、英语 wise 和 wit、德语 wissen——看见就是知道。拉丁语的分词 visus 给了 visio、visit(去看)、advise(ad-visum,把看法给你)。pro-videre 是提前看(provide 本义\"预见并准备\";providence 是上帝的预见),in-videre 是斜眼看(invidia 嫉妒,英语 envy),e-videns 是\"看得出来\"(evident)。视频 video 直接就是拉丁语\"我看见\"。",
    "videre (to see) is PIE *weid- — to see and therefore to know — the root of Greek idea (a seen form), of historia (Greek histōr, one who has seen, a witness), of English wise and wit, of German wissen: seeing is knowing. The participle visus gave visio, visit (going to see), advise (ad-visum, giving you my view). pro-videre is seeing ahead (provide meant to foresee and prepare; providence is God's foresight), in-videre is looking askance (invidia, envy), e-videns is what can be seen out — evident. Video is simply Latin for \"I see\"."],
  reg: ["regere(引导、统治)的本义是\"划直线\":印欧语 *reg- 表示伸直、指方向,英语 right(直的、正确的)、德语 recht、Reich 都同源。rex 是\"给部落指方向的人\"——但罗马人赶走末代国王(前 509 年)后再也不肯用这个头衔。rectus 是直的(correct 是拉直,direct 是直指,erect 是竖直)。regula 是木匠的直尺,后来指规则、修会会规(rule);regio 是占卜官用手在天上划出的\"方向区\",后来才指地区。region、regime、regent、regular——全是同一条直线。",
    "regere (to guide, rule) originally meant to draw a straight line: PIE *reg-, to straighten, to point the way, the root of English right, German recht and Reich. A rex was the man who set the tribe's direction — though after expelling their last king in 509 BC the Romans refused the title forever. rectus is straight (correct straightens, direct points straight, erect stands straight). A regula was the carpenter's straight-edge, then a rule, then a monastic Rule; a regio was the strip of sky an augur marked out with his staff, only later a region. Region, regime, regent, regular — one straight line."],
  fin: ["finis 是罗马农田的界石。前 5 世纪《十二表法》就规定邻田之间要留五尺界带,界神 Terminus 受人祭拜;finis 最初就是这条\"边界\",后来才引申为\"尽头、结束\"。de-finire 是把界线划清楚(定义就是画边界),con-finis 是共用一条界的(confine,邻界;后来指关起来),af-finis 是\"界线挨着\"(姻亲 affinity),in-finitus 是没有边界的。中世纪 finis 又指\"付清了结\"——finance 本义是了结债务的钱,fine(罚款)是了结案子的钱。",
    "finis was the boundary stone of a Roman field. The Twelve Tables (5th century BC) already required a five-foot strip between neighbours' land, guarded by the god Terminus; finis first meant that border and only later the end of anything. de-finire is drawing the line clearly (a definition is a boundary), con-finis shares a border (confine, later to shut in), af-finis has an adjoining border (affinity — in-laws), in-finitus has no border at all. In the Middle Ages finis also meant a settling of accounts: finance was money that ended a debt, a fine money that ended a case."],
  gen: ["*genh₁-(生、产)是印欧语最丰产的词根:拉丁语 gignere(生)、genus(种、族)、gens(氏族)、natura 里的 gnatus(生出的);希腊语 genos、genesis;英语 kin、kind、king(\"族人\"之首)。罗马公民的名字有三段,中间那段 nomen 标氏族(gens):Gaius Julius Caesar 属于 Julia 氏族。generosus 是\"出身好的\",慷慨 generous 是贵族该有的气度;genius 是每个人出生时随身的守护神;in-genium 是生来就有的才能(engine 最早指天才的发明)。基因 gene 是 1909 年造的新词,但根是六千年前的。",
    "*genh₁- (to beget, be born) is the most fertile Indo-European root: Latin gignere, genus (kind, stock), gens (clan), the gnatus hidden in natura; Greek genos, genesis; English kin, kind, king (head of the kin). A Roman citizen's middle name, the nomen, marked his gens: Gaius Julius Caesar belonged to the gens Julia. generosus meant well-born, so generous was the bearing expected of a noble; a genius was the guardian spirit born with each person; in-genium was inborn talent (an engine was first a work of genius). Gene was coined in 1909, but its root is six thousand years old."],
  fer: ["ferre(带、承受)是拉丁语最不规则的动词——现在时 fero,完成时 tuli,分词 latus,三个形式来自三个不同的古词根,所以同一家族里既有 -fer 又有 -lat:trans-fer 与 trans-late(把意思\"搬\"过去),re-fer 与 re-late,con-fer 与 col-late。印欧语 *bʰer- 在英语里是 bear(承受、生育),希腊语是 pherein(metaphor 是\"搬过去\"的比喻)。罗马人说 fertilis(能\"生出\"庄稼的),说 Lucifer(带光者,晨星),说 suf-ferre(从下面承受,suffer)。差异 differ 本义\"分开搬走\"。",
    "ferre (to carry, bear) is Latin's most irregular verb — present fero, perfect tuli, participle latus come from three different ancient roots, which is why one family holds both -fer and -lat: trans-fer and trans-late (carrying meaning across), re-fer and re-late, con-fer and col-late. PIE *bʰer- is English bear (to carry, to give birth) and Greek pherein (a metaphor carries meaning over). Romans spoke of fertile land that bears crops, of Lucifer the light-bearer (the morning star), of suf-ferre, bearing from beneath — to suffer. To differ is literally to carry apart."],
  ten: ["tenere(握、持)与 tendere 同根(*ten-,拉紧):握住就是拉住不放。罗马地主 tenet 田产,佃户 tenant 是\"持有\"土地的人(法语 tenir 的分词)。con-tinere 是\"一起握住\"——容器 container 装着,大陆 continent 是连成一片的陆地,continue 是一直握着不放;re-tinere 留住,sus-tinere 从下面托住(sustain),ab-s-tinere 把手缩回(abstain),per-tinere 一直延伸到(pertain)。tenor 原是歌里\"一直持续\"的主旋律声部,tenacious 是抓紧不放的,tenet 是\"他持有(的信条)\"。",
    "tenere (to hold) shares the root *ten- (to stretch) with tendere: holding is stretching and not letting go. A Roman landowner tenet his estate; a tenant (French tenir) is one who holds land. con-tinere is holding together — a container holds, a continent is land held in one piece, to continue is to keep holding; re-tinere keeps back, sus-tinere holds up from below (sustain), abs-tinere pulls the hand away (abstain), per-tinere reaches all the way to (pertain). The tenor was the voice that held the melody through a song, tenacious means holding fast, and a tenet is \"he holds\" — a belief held."],
  mov: ["movere(动)来自印欧语 *meu̯h₁-,分词 motus 给了 motion、motor、moment(momentum,\"动一下的力\")。罗马人用它说很实际的事:军队 movere castra 是拔营,商人 movere 货物;也说心动:com-motus(commotion,情绪被一起搅动)、e-motio(被搅出来的情绪——emotion 这个词到 17 世纪才从法语进入英语,本义\"骚动\")。pro-movere 向前推(晋升 promote),re-movere 挪走。mobilis 是能动的,罗马人说 mobile vulgus(善变的群众)——mob 就是这个词的缩写,1680 年代伦敦人嫌 mobile 太长砍成了 mob。",
    "movere (to move) comes from PIE *meu̯h₁-; its participle motus gave motion, motor, moment (momentum, the force of a single move). Romans used it for practical things — an army movere castra breaks camp, a merchant moves goods — and for the heart: com-motus (commotion, feelings stirred together), e-motio (feeling stirred out; emotion entered English from French only in the 17th century, meaning a disturbance). pro-movere pushes forward (promote), re-movere shifts away. mobilis is what can move; Romans spoke of the mobile vulgus, the fickle crowd — and Londoners of the 1680s clipped mobile to mob."],
  form: ["forma(形、模子)可能是从希腊语 morphē 借来并调了音的词。罗马陶匠和鞋匠都有 forma——模具、鞋楦;学校老师教 formare 字母的形状;法学家把诉讼固定成 formula(小模子,固定句式)——今天的公式、配方就是从法庭套话来的。in-formare 是\"把形状印进心里\"——告知(information 本义是塑造心智);re-formare 重塑;con-formare 塑成一样(conform);uni-formis 只有一种样子(制服)。formosus 是\"形状美的\"——西班牙语 hermoso、葡萄牙语 formoso(福尔摩沙 Formosa,\"美丽岛\")。",
    "forma (shape, mould) may be Greek morphē borrowed with its sounds shuffled. Roman potters and shoemakers each had a forma — a mould, a last; schoolmasters taught pupils to formare letters; jurists fixed lawsuits into a formula, a little mould of set words — today's formulas and recipes descend from courtroom phrasing. in-formare is pressing a shape into the mind — to inform (information once meant shaping the mind); re-formare reshapes; con-formare shapes alike; uni-formis has a single shape (a uniform). formosus meant shapely — Spanish hermoso, Portuguese formoso, the island of Formosa."],
  pos: ["ponere(放)的分词 positus 是这一族的主干:position 是放的位置,positive 原义\"放定的、确定的\"(法学里 lex positiva 是人定的法)。com-ponere 把东西放在一起(compose 作曲、compound 化合物),de-ponere 放下(deposit 存款、depose 罢黜),ex-ponere 放出来给人看(expose、exhibition 的 exposition),pro-ponere 放到前面(propose 提议),sup-ponere 放在底下当前提(suppose 假设),op-ponere 放在对面(oppose)。法语把 ponere 变成 poser,又和希腊语 pausis(停)混了,于是 pose、pause 在英语里长得像亲戚。",
    "The participle positus of ponere (to put) is the trunk of this family: a position is where something is put, positive first meant \"set down, settled\" (in law, lex positiva is law laid down by men). com-ponere puts things together (compose, compound), de-ponere puts down (deposit, depose), ex-ponere puts out on view (expose, exposition), pro-ponere puts forward (propose), sup-ponere puts underneath as a premise (suppose), op-ponere puts opposite (oppose). French turned ponere into poser and tangled it with Greek pausis (a stop), which is why pose and pause look like relatives in English."],
  tract: ["trahere(拉、拖)的分词 tractus 是这一族的根。罗马农夫用 tractus 说犁拖出的\"一道\",后来指一片拖长的土地(tract);医生说 tractare 是\"反复拉、揉\"——治疗、处理,所以 treat、treaty(反复谈判出的条约)、tractate(论文)都从\"揉\"来。at-trahere 拉向自己(attract),con-trahere 拉紧到一起(contract:肌肉收缩,也指双方拉到一起的合同),dis-trahere 拉向两边(distract 分心),ex-trahere 拉出来(extract),sub-trahere 从下面抽走(subtract),ab-s-trahere 拉离具体事物(abstract)。拖拉机 tractor 是 1900 年前后美国造的词,但动作还是拉。",
    "The participle tractus of trahere (to pull, drag) roots this family. A Roman farmer's tractus was the stroke a plough dragged, then a stretch of land (a tract); a doctor's tractare was to pull and knead repeatedly — to treat, hence treat, treaty (an agreement kneaded out in talks) and tractate. at-trahere pulls toward you (attract), con-trahere pulls tight together (contract: a muscle, or two parties), dis-trahere pulls apart (distract), ex-trahere pulls out (extract), sub-trahere draws from beneath (subtract), abs-trahere pulls away from the concrete (abstract). Tractor was coined in America around 1900, but the motion is still pulling."],
  dic: ["dicere(说)来自印欧语 *deik-(指出、显示),同根的英语词是 teach 和 token,希腊语是 dikē(指出的正道、正义)。在拉丁语里\"说\"从一开始就带着权威:iu-dex(判官)是\"说出法律的人\",dictator 是\"发号施令者\"——共和国的紧急职位,后来才成为独裁者;in-dicare 指出(index 是指出的手指、索引),prae-dicere 预先说(predict),bene-dicere 说好话(祝福),male-dicere 咒骂,con-dicio 是双方\"一起说定\"的条件(condition)。dictum 是名言,verdict 是\"真话\"(vere dictum)。",
    "dicere (to say) comes from PIE *deik- (to point out, show), the root of English teach and token and of Greek dikē (the way pointed out, justice). In Latin, saying carried authority from the start: a iu-dex is one who speaks the law, a dictator one who issues commands — an emergency office of the Republic before it meant a tyrant; in-dicare points out (an index is the pointing finger), prae-dicere says beforehand (predict), bene-dicere speaks well (to bless), male-dicere curses, con-dicio is what two parties say together — a condition. A dictum is a saying; a verdict is what is truly said, vere dictum."],
  fac: ["facere(做、造)来自印欧语 *dʰeh₁-(放置),同根的英语词是 do,希腊语是 tithēmi(放)、thesis。它是拉丁语里最能干的动词,几乎能和一切前缀搭配,而且尾音会变:单独是 fac-,在复合词里变 -fic-(ef-ficere 造成,effect;suf-ficere 够用,sufficient),分词变 -fect-(per-fectus 彻底做完的,perfect;in-fectus 被\"做进去\"的,infect)。facilis 是\"容易做的\"(facility 设施是让事情容易做的东西),dif-ficilis 是难做的,fac-tum 是\"做成的事\"——事实;manu-factura 是手工做的,现在反倒指机器造的。",
    "facere (to do, make) comes from PIE *dʰeh₁- (to set, place), the root of English do and Greek tithēmi (to put) and thesis. It is Latin's busiest verb, combining with almost every prefix and changing its vowel as it goes: fac- alone, -fic- inside compounds (ef-ficere to bring about, effect; suf-ficere to be enough, sufficient), -fect- in the participle (per-fectus done through and through, perfect; in-fectus done into, infected). facilis is easy to do (a facility makes things easy), dif-ficilis hard to do, fac-tum a thing done — a fact; manu-factura was made by hand, and now means made by machine."],
  voc: ["vox(声音)与 vocare(呼叫)来自印欧语 *wekʷ-(说话),同根的希腊语是 epos(话、史诗),梵语 vac(言说)。罗马人 ad-vocare 是\"把人叫到身边帮忙\"——律师 advocate 就是被叫来的人;con-vocare 召集(convocation),e-vocare 唤出(evoke,也指唤出亡灵),pro-vocare 喊出来挑衅(provoke),re-vocare 叫回(revoke)。vocatio 本义\"呼召\",中世纪指上帝召人去当修士,后来指职业(vocation)——职业是被叫去做的事。vocabulum 是\"叫出来的名\",vowel(vocalis)是能出声的字母。",
    "vox (voice) and vocare (to call) come from PIE *wekʷ- (to speak), which also gave Greek epos (word, epic) and Sanskrit vac. Romans ad-vocare when they called someone to their side for help — an advocate is the one called in; con-vocare summons (convocation), e-vocare calls out (evoke, also of calling up the dead), pro-vocare calls forth a challenge (provoke), re-vocare calls back (revoke). A vocatio was a calling; in the Middle Ages God's call to the cloister, later any profession — a vocation is what one is called to do. A vocabulum is a name called out; a vowel (vocalis) is a letter that sounds."],
  grad: ["gradus(步、台阶)来自 gradi(走),印欧语 *gʰredʰ-。罗马军团按 gradus 前进,竞技场的座位是一级级 gradus,官员的升迁叫 gradus honorum——荣誉的阶梯。分词 gressus 给了另一半家族:ag-gredi 走向(aggression 是主动逼近),con-gressus 走到一起(congress 会议),pro-gressus 向前走(progress),re-gressus 退回,di-gressio 走岔了(digression 离题),trans-gredi 跨过线(transgress)。degree 是法语磨过的 de-gradus(一级),graduate 是走完全部台阶的人,gradual 是一级一级来的。",
    "gradus (a step, a stair) comes from gradi (to walk), PIE *gʰredʰ-. Roman legions advanced by the gradus, arena seats rose in gradus, an official's career climbed the gradus honorum — the ladder of honours. The participle gressus gave the other half of the family: ag-gredi to step toward (aggression is closing in), con-gressus stepping together (congress), pro-gressus stepping forward, re-gressus stepping back, di-gressio stepping aside (digression), trans-gredi stepping over the line. Degree is de-gradus worn down by French, a graduate has climbed every step, and gradual goes one step at a time."],
  pend: ["pendere(挂)与 pendere(称重、付钱)是同一个词:罗马早期没有铸币,买东西是把青铜块挂上天平称——所以\"挂\"就是\"称\",称了就是\"付\"。pensum 是女奴每天被称好的羊毛定额(后来指任务),stipendium 是士兵的军饷(称给你的),ex-pendere 是称出去(expense、spend),com-pensare 是放在天平两边抵平(compensate),dis-pensare 是分着称给各人(dispense)。悬着的 pendulus 给了 pendulum、pending、suspend;pensare(反复称量)在法语里变成 penser——思考就是在心里称重,pensive 是若有所思。",
    "pendere (to hang) and pendere (to weigh, to pay) are one word: early Rome had no coins, so buying meant hanging lumps of bronze on the scales — to hang was to weigh, and to weigh was to pay. A pensum was the daily weight of wool measured out to a slave woman (later any task), a stipendium the soldier's weighed-out pay, ex-pendere weighing out (expense, spend), com-pensare balancing the two pans (compensate), dis-pensare weighing out shares (dispense). Hanging pendulus gave pendulum, pending, suspend; pensare, to weigh repeatedly, became French penser — to think is to weigh in the mind, and pensive is thoughtful."],
  viv: ["vivere(活)来自印欧语 *gʷeih₃-(生),同根有希腊语 bios(生命,bio-)、zōē(zoo)、英语 quick(古义\"活的\",the quick and the dead)。罗马人喝酒举杯说 Vive!(祝你活着),分词 victus 指\"活命的食物\"(victuals)。vita 是生命(vital、vitamin 是 1912 年造的\"生命胺\"),vivus 是活的(vivid 是生动到像活的),con-vivium 是一起活着——宴会(convivial),super-vivere 是比别人活得久(survive),re-vivere 复活(revive)。中世纪法语的 vivre 给英语留下 viand(食物)和 victuals——\"活\"首先是\"有吃的\"。",
    "vivere (to live) comes from PIE *gʷeih₃- (to live), also the root of Greek bios (bio-), zōē (zoo) and English quick (originally \"alive\": the quick and the dead). Romans raised their cups with Vive! (may you live); the participle victus meant the food one lives on (victuals). vita is life (vital; vitamin was coined in 1912 as \"life amine\"), vivus alive (vivid is lifelike), con-vivium living together — a banquet (convivial), super-vivere outliving others (survive), re-vivere coming back to life (revive). Medieval French vivre left English viand and victuals — living was first of all having something to eat."],
  sequ: ["sequi(跟随)来自印欧语 *sekʷ-,同根词有希腊语 hepomai(跟随)和英语 see(视线\"跟着\"东西走)——以及 second:secundus 是\"跟在第一后面的\",也指顺风顺水(secundus ventus,\"跟着你的风\"),所以 second 和 favourable 曾是一个词。分词 secutus 给了 con-sequentia(跟着来的后果)、per-sequi(一路追到底,迫害 persecute)、pro-sequi(跟着继续,起诉 prosecute)、ex-sequi(跟到最后办完,execute——也就是处决)。sect 是跟随某人的一派,suit(法语 suite)是跟班随从,后来指一套跟着穿的衣服。",
    "sequi (to follow) comes from PIE *sekʷ-, shared with Greek hepomai and English see (the eye follows a thing) — and with second: secundus is what follows the first, also a following wind (secundus ventus), so second and favourable were once one word. The participle secutus gave con-sequentia (what follows, consequence), per-sequi (to follow to the end, persecute), pro-sequi (to follow onward, prosecute), ex-sequi (to follow through to completion, execute — including a death sentence). A sect follows a leader; a suit (French suite) was a retinue that followed, then a set of clothes that go together."],
  sent: ["sentire(感觉、察觉)来自印欧语 *sent-(走一条路、找方向),德语 sinnen(思索)、Sinn(感官、意义)是它的日耳曼亲戚:感觉就是心在找路。罗马元老发表意见叫 sententiam dicere(说出自己的\"感觉\"),sententia 后来指判决、格言(sentence)。sensus 是感官,也指\"意思\";con-sentire 一起感觉(consent 同意),dis-sentire 感觉不一(dissent),re-sentire 反复感到(法语 ressentir,英语 resent 只留下了怨恨这一种)。sentinel(哨兵)是意大利语 sentinella,\"负责察觉的人\"。",
    "sentire (to feel, perceive) comes from PIE *sent- (to take a path, find a direction); German sinnen (to ponder) and Sinn (sense, meaning) are its Germanic cousins — to feel is the mind finding its way. A Roman senator gave his opinion by sententiam dicere, speaking his \"feeling\"; sententia then meant a judgement or a maxim (sentence). sensus is a sense and also a meaning; con-sentire is feeling together (consent), dis-sentire feeling apart (dissent), re-sentire feeling again (French ressentir; English resent kept only the bitter kind). A sentinel is Italian sentinella, the one whose job is to perceive."],
  mitt: ["mittere(送、放出)在古拉丁语里先是\"放开、扔\":放出猎犬、投出标枪(missile 就是\"可投掷的\")。放开一个人就是 dimittere(遣散、放走,后来的 dismiss),放出使者就是 missio(派遣;传教 mission 是被派出去的人)。ad-mittere 放进来(admit),com-mittere 交给、也指\"做下\"罪行(commit),o-mittere 放过去不管(omit),per-mittere 放行(permit),pro-mittere 放到前面去——承诺(promise),sub-mittere 放到下面(submit),trans-mittere 送过去(transmit)。mass(弥撒)来自礼毕的一句 Ite, missa est(去吧,散会了)。",
    "mittere (to send, let go) first meant to release or throw in early Latin: loosing the hounds, hurling a spear (a missile is what can be thrown). Letting a person go is dimittere (dismiss); sending out an envoy is a missio (a mission is those sent out). ad-mittere lets in (admit), com-mittere hands over — and also \"does\" a crime (commit), o-mittere lets pass (omit), per-mittere lets through (permit), pro-mittere sends forward — a promise, sub-mittere puts under (submit), trans-mittere sends across. The Mass takes its name from the closing words Ite, missa est — go, it is the dismissal."],
  solv: ["solvere(松开)是 se-(分开)+ luere(松、洗、赎)的合成,印欧语 *leu-(松开),同根的英语词是 loose 和 lose。罗马人 solvere 缆绳出港,solvere 欠债(付清就是\"解开\"债务——solvent 是还得起的,今天也指溶剂),solvere 谜题(solution)。ab-solvere 是法官宣判\"解开\"被告的罪(absolve),后来教会用它说赦罪;re-solvere 彻底解开(resolve 解决、下决心),dis-solvere 化开(dissolve)。absolute 本义\"完全松开、不受约束的\"——绝对权力就是不受任何绳子牵制的权力。",
    "solvere (to loosen) is se- (apart) + luere (to loose, wash, redeem), PIE *leu-, the root of English loose and lose. Romans solvere the mooring ropes, solvere a debt (paying it off unties it — solvent means able to pay, and now a liquid that unties solids), solvere a riddle (solution). ab-solvere is the judge loosening the defendant from the charge (absolve), later the Church's loosening from sin; re-solvere unties thoroughly (resolve), dis-solvere loosens into liquid (dissolve). absolute meant wholly untied, bound by nothing — absolute power answers to no rope."],
  man: ["manus(手)来自印欧语 *man-,同根词很少却很关键:古英语 mund(保护,\"手的庇护\"),古挪威语 mund。在罗马法里 manus 是家长对妻子儿女的\"手中之权\":把奴隶从手里放开叫 manu-mittere(解放),把权利交到别人手里叫 mandare(man-dare,交托——mandate、command、demand、recommend 全在此)。manu-scriptum 手写,manu-factura 手做,manu-alis 手用的(manual)。maintain 是法语 main-tenir(用手扶住),manage 是意大利语 maneggiare(用手驯马),manner 是 manuarius(手的方式)——做事的方式。",
    "manus (hand) comes from PIE *man-; its few cognates matter: Old English mund (protection, the shelter of a hand), Old Norse mund. In Roman law manus was the father's power \"in hand\" over wife and children: releasing a slave from the hand is manu-mittere (manumission), placing something in another's hand is mandare (man-dare, to entrust — mandate, command, demand, recommend). manu-scriptum is handwritten, manu-factura handmade, manu-alis for the hand (manual). maintain is French main-tenir (to hold with the hand), manage Italian maneggiare (to handle a horse), manner manuarius — the way of the hand, the way a thing is done."],
  part: ["pars(部分)来自印欧语 *per-(分配、给予),同根的希腊语是 poros(通道)和 eporon(给),拉丁语的 parare(准备)、portio(份额)。罗马人分战利品叫 partiri,分家产叫 partitio;par-ticeps 是\"拿了一份的人\"(参与者 participant),par-ticula 是小份(粒子 particle),partialis 是只顾一部分的(偏袒 partial)。party 最初是\"一方\"(法语 partie),16 世纪才指政党,18 世纪才指聚会;partner 是共同持份的人;apart 是各占一部分;de-partire 是分开走(depart,部门 department 是分出的一块)。",
    "pars (a part) comes from PIE *per- (to allot, give), shared with Greek poros (a passage) and eporon (gave), and with Latin parare (to prepare) and portio (a share). Romans partiri booty and partitio an inheritance; a par-ticeps is one who took a share (participant), a par-ticula a small share (particle), partialis mindful of one part only (partial). A party was first one side (French partie), a political party only in the 16th century, a social gathering in the 18th; a partner holds a share; apart is each in its own part; de-partire is to go separate ways (depart; a department is a piece cut off)."],
  val: ["valere(强壮、有力)来自印欧语 *wal-(强),同根的英语词是 wield(掌控),德语 walten(统治)。罗马人告别说 Vale!(愿你强健)——信尾写 vale 就像今天的\"保重\"。valor 先指强健,后指勇武;valere 也指\"值多少\"(强到什么程度),于是有 value、valid(有力的、有效的)、in-valid(无力的——伤兵)、prae-valere 力量胜过(prevail)、e-valuare 估出值(evaluate)、aequi-valens 等值(equivalent)。con-valescere 是\"逐渐变强\"——康复期 convalescence 就是重新有力量。",
    "valere (to be strong, to be well) comes from PIE *wal- (strong), the root of English wield and German walten (to rule). Romans said goodbye with Vale! — be strong — and closed letters with it, like \"take care\". valor first meant strength, then bravery; valere also meant to be worth so much (to be strong to that degree), hence value, valid (having force), in-valid (without strength — a wounded soldier), prae-valere (to prevail), e-valuare (to draw out the worth, evaluate), aequi-valens (equal in worth). con-valescere is growing strong again — convalescence is strength coming back."],
  scrib: ["scribere(写)的本义是\"刻、划\":罗马小学生用铁笔在蜡板上划字,划错了用扁头抹平。印欧语 *(s)ker-(刻)在希腊语里是 skariphaomai(划刻),英语 scratch、share(切开)也远亲。scriba 是书记官,scriptum 是写下的东西(scripture 后来专指经文);de-scribere 是\"把样子写下来\"(describe),in-scribere 刻在上面(碑铭 inscription),prae-scribere 事先写好规定(prescribe:医生的处方、法律的规定),sub-scribere 在下面签名(subscribe 本义签字认购),pro-scribere 是公告没收——苏拉和三巨头的 proscriptio 名单上的人可以被合法杀死。",
    "scribere (to write) originally meant to scratch, incise: Roman schoolboys scratched letters into wax with a stylus and smoothed mistakes with its flat end. PIE *(s)ker- (to cut) also gave Greek skariphaomai (to scratch) and, distantly, English scratch and share. A scriba was a clerk, a scriptum a thing written (scripture later meant holy writ); de-scribere writes down how something looks (describe), in-scribere carves on (inscription), prae-scribere writes down beforehand (prescribe — a doctor's or the law's), sub-scribere signs underneath (subscribe once meant signing to buy), pro-scribere posts a public notice of confiscation — anyone on Sulla's or the triumvirs' proscriptio list could be killed lawfully."],
  terr: ["terra(土地、干地)可能来自 *ters-(干),同根的英语词是 thirst(渴)、德语 dürr(干旱):对早期印欧人来说,大地首先是\"干的那部分\"(与 mare 海相对)。罗马人 terra 与 mare 并举:terra marique(海陆),Mediterraneum mare 是\"陆地中间的海\"。territorium 是一座城管辖的土地,terrestris 是地上的,sub-terraneus 地下的,terra-cotta 是意大利语\"烤过的土\";terrace 是意大利语 terrazza,一层层堆起的土台。terrier 犬是法语 chien terrier,钻地洞追獾的狗。",
    "terra (earth, dry land) probably comes from *ters- (dry), the root of English thirst and German dürr (parched): to early Indo-Europeans the earth was first of all the dry part, as against mare, the sea. Romans paired them — terra marique, by land and sea — and called the Mediterranean the sea in the middle of the land. A territorium was the land a town governed, terrestris earthly, sub-terraneus underground, terra-cotta Italian for baked earth; a terrace is Italian terrazza, earth heaped in tiers. The terrier is the French chien terrier, the dog that goes to earth after badgers."],
  sacr: ["sacer(神圣的)来自印欧语 *sak-(使受约束、献出),同根有拉丁语 sancire(用宗教仪式确立)——sanctus(圣的)、sanction(确立,后来也指惩罚)。sacer 在罗马有双重含义:献给神的东西是神圣的,而被神诅咒、任何人都可杀的罪人也叫 sacer(homo sacer)。sacer-dos 是\"献圣物的人\"(祭司),sacri-ficium 是\"做成圣的事\"(祭祀),sacra-mentum 是士兵入伍的誓言,后来指基督教圣礼;con-secrare 献给神,ex-secrari 是从圣中除出——诅咒(execrate);se-cretum 与此无关(那是 cernere 的分离),但 sacristy、sexton(sacristanus 的英语磨损)都是圣器室的看守。",
    "sacer (holy) comes from PIE *sak- (to bind, to dedicate), shared with Latin sancire (to make binding by rite) — sanctus (holy), sanction (a ratification, later a penalty). sacer was double-edged in Rome: what was given to the gods was sacred, but a criminal cursed by the gods, whom anyone might kill, was also sacer (homo sacer). A sacer-dos is one who gives holy things (priest), a sacri-ficium a making-holy (sacrifice), a sacra-mentum the soldier's binding oath, later the Christian sacrament; con-secrare dedicates, ex-secrari casts out of the holy — to execrate. The sexton is sacristanus worn down by English: keeper of the sacred vessels."],
  sal: ["sal(盐)是最古老的欧洲词之一(希腊语 hals,英语 salt,德语 Salz)。罗马从台伯河口的盐田通往内陆的路叫 Via Salaria(盐路),士兵领的\"盐钱\"叫 salarium——salary 就是买盐的钱。salsus 是咸的(sauce、salsa、sausage 都是腌过的),salata 是腌菜(salad 本义\"加了盐的\"),saline 盐水。另一个 sal- 家族来自 salvus(平安、完好)与 salus(健康、拯救):salve、salutare(问候,祝你健康,salute)、salvatio(救赎)、safe 是法语磨损的 salvus。两个 sal 无关,但罗马人也把盐当健康的象征。",
    "sal (salt) is among Europe's oldest words (Greek hals, English salt, German Salz). The road from the salt pans at the Tiber's mouth inland was the Via Salaria, and the soldier's salt-allowance was the salarium — a salary is salt money. salsus is salted (sauce, salsa, sausage are all cured), salata a salted dish (salad means \"salted\"), saline brine. A second sal- family comes from salvus (safe, whole) and salus (health, deliverance): salve, salutare (to greet by wishing health, salute), salvatio (salvation), and safe is salvus worn down by French. The two roots are unrelated, though Romans too saw salt as health."],
  lev: ["levis(轻)来自印欧语 *h₁lengʷʰ-,同根的英语词是 light(轻)、lung(肺是轻得能浮在水上的器官)。levare 是\"使变轻\"——举起:罗马人 levare 锚,levare 军队(征兵 levy 是\"举起\"一支军队),e-levare 举高(elevator),re-levare 减轻(relieve;relief 先是减轻痛苦,后指浮雕——从平面上\"举起\"的部分),al-leviare 减轻,levitas 轻浮,leverage 是用杠杆(lever,法语 levier)举重物。意大利语 lievito 和法语 levain 是让面团\"升起\"的酵母。",
    "levis (light in weight) comes from PIE *h₁lengʷʰ-, the root of English light and lung (the organ light enough to float). levare is to make light — to lift: Romans levare the anchor and levare an army (a levy is an army raised), e-levare lifts up (elevator), re-levare lightens (relieve; relief was first eased pain, then sculpture raised from a flat ground), al-leviare eases, levitas is lightness of character, and leverage lifts weights with a lever (French levier). Italian lievito and French levain are the yeast that makes dough rise."],
  curr: ["currere(跑)来自印欧语 *kers-,同根有英语 horse(跑的动物)和德语 Ross。罗马赛车叫 currus,跑道叫 cursus(course 课程本义\"跑一趟的路\"),奔跑的信使叫 cursor(光标 cursor 是屏幕上跑来跑去的记号)。con-currere 一起跑(concur 同意;竞争 concurrence 是跑到一处),oc-currere 跑到面前(occur 发生),re-currere 跑回来(recur),ex-cursio 跑出去(远足),dis-cursus 跑来跑去——谈话(discourse)。current 是\"正在跑的\"(水流、电流、时事),currency 是在市面上跑的钱;corridor 是意大利语 corridore,跑道般的走廊;courier 是跑腿送信的。",
    "currere (to run) comes from PIE *kers-, the root of English horse (the running animal) and German Ross. A Roman racing chariot was a currus, the track a cursus (a course is a run), a running messenger a cursor (the cursor runs about the screen). con-currere is running together (concur; concurrence, competition, is running to the same place), oc-currere runs up to meet you (occur), re-currere runs back (recur), ex-cursio is a run out (excursion), dis-cursus running to and fro — conversation (discourse). current is what is running (water, electricity, events), currency money that runs through the market; a corridor is Italian corridore, a running-place; a courier runs with letters."],
  spec: ["specere(看)来自印欧语 *spek-,同根的希腊语是 skopein(看,scope、-scopy——希腊语把 p 和 k 换了位置),德语 spähen(窥探),英语 spy。罗马人用它造了一整套\"看\"的词:spectare 观看(spectacle 演出,spectator 观众),spec-ies 是\"看上去的样子\"——外观,后来指物种,speculum 镜子,specimen 样本(给人看的),con-spicuus 显眼的,in-spicere 检查,per-spicere 看穿(perspective 透视),su-spicere 抬头看、也指怀疑(suspect),re-spicere 回头看——尊重 respect 是\"回头再看一眼\"。speculari 是站在高处瞭望(speculate 后来指投机——远远地看行情)。",
    "specere (to look) comes from PIE *spek-, which Greek turned into skopein (scope, -scopy — Greek swapped the p and k), German into spähen (to spy out), English into spy. Romans built a whole vocabulary of looking from it: spectare to watch (spectacle, spectator), spec-ies what a thing looks like — appearance, later a species, speculum a mirror, specimen a thing shown, con-spicuus eye-catching, in-spicere to examine, per-spicere to see through (perspective), su-spicere to look up at — or to suspect, re-spicere to look back — respect is a second look. speculari was watching from a height; to speculate came to mean watching the market from afar."],
  cred: ["credere(信)是印欧语 *ḱred-dʰeh₁-(把心放上去)的合成词:*ḱred 是心(与 cor、heart 同根),*dʰeh₁- 是放(与 facere、do 同根)。梵语 śraddhā(信仰)是同一个合成。所以\"相信\"的本义是把心交给谁。罗马商人 credere 是\"赊给\"——把钱交托出去:creditor 债主,creditum 借款,credit 信用。基督徒念的信经开头是 Credo(我信)——creed 就是这个词。cred-ibilis 可信的,in-credibilis 难以置信,cred-ulus 轻信的;credential 是让人相信你的凭证。",
    "credere (to believe) is an Indo-European compound *ḱred-dʰeh₁- — to put one's heart: *ḱred is heart (cognate with cor and heart), *dʰeh₁- is to put (cognate with facere and do). Sanskrit śraddhā (faith) is the same compound. To believe is literally to hand over your heart. A Roman merchant's credere was lending on trust: creditor, creditum (a loan), credit. The Christian profession of faith opens with Credo, I believe — the creed. cred-ibilis is believable, in-credibilis beyond belief, cred-ulus too ready to believe; a credential is what makes people believe you."],
  pass: ["passus(步)来自 pandere(张开):一步就是两腿张开一次。罗马的里程单位 mille passuum(一千步,约 1.48 公里)就是 mile 的来源——一\"步\"是双步,左右脚各一次。晚期拉丁语造了动词 passare(一步步走过去),它成了法语 passer、意大利语 passare、西班牙语 pasar 和英语 pass:passage 通道,passenger 过路人,passport 是\"通过港口/城门\"的许可,trespass 是跨过界线,surpass 超过,compass 是\"用脚步量圆\"(法语 compasser)——圆规和指南针都从量步来。pace 是 passus 经法语的形状。",
    "passus (a pace) comes from pandere (to spread): a step is the legs opened once. Rome's mile was mille passuum, a thousand paces (about 1.48 km) — a passus being a double step, left foot and right. Late Latin coined the verb passare, to go step by step, which became French passer, Italian passare, Spanish pasar and English pass: a passage, a passenger, a passport (leave to pass the port or gate), trespass (stepping over a line), surpass, and compass — French compasser, to measure a circle by paces, hence both the drawing tool and the direction-finder. Pace is passus in its French shape."],
  mand: ["mandare 是 manus(手)+ dare(给)的合成:交到某人手里。罗马法里 mandatum 是委托合同——你交托我办事,我不收钱;皇帝的诏令也叫 mandatum。com-mendare 是郑重托付(commend 称赞、recommend 推荐,都是把人交托给别人照看),de-mandare 是交办(法语 demander 变成\"要求\",英语 demand),mandatory 是被委托而必须做的。command 是 com-mandare 经法语的形状,commander、commandment(十诫)随之而来;countermand 是收回命令。mandarin(官员)与此无关,那是梵语 mantrin(顾问)经马来语、葡萄牙语来的。",
    "mandare is manus (hand) + dare (to give): to put into someone's hand. In Roman law a mandatum was a contract of trust — you entrust me with a task, I take no fee; an emperor's instruction was also a mandatum. com-mendare entrusts solemnly (commend, recommend — placing a person in another's care), de-mandare hands over a task (French demander became \"to ask\", English demand), mandatory is what one is charged with doing. command is com-mandare in its French shape, with commander and commandment (the ten) behind it; countermand takes the order back. Mandarin is unrelated — Sanskrit mantrin (counsellor) via Malay and Portuguese."],
  vinc: ["vincere(征服)来自印欧语 *weik-(战斗),同根的古英语 wigan(打仗)、古挪威语 vig。凯撒在泽拉战役后给元老院的捷报只有三个词:Veni, vidi, vici——来了,看了,赢了。victor 胜者,victoria 胜利女神(维多利亚),in-victus 不可战胜(罗马人称太阳神 Sol Invictus),con-vincere 是在辩论中\"彻底打败\"对方(convince 说服;convict 是被证据打败的人),e-vincere 打赢并驱逐(evict),pro-vincia 是\"被打赢的地盘\"(行省)。Vincent 这个名字意为\"正在得胜的\"。",
    "vincere (to conquer) comes from PIE *weik- (to fight), the root of Old English wigan (to make war) and Old Norse vig. Caesar's dispatch to the Senate after Zela was three words: Veni, vidi, vici — I came, I saw, I conquered. victor is the winner, victoria the goddess of victory (Victoria), in-victus unconquered (Rome's Sol Invictus), con-vincere is beating someone thoroughly in argument (convince; a convict is one beaten by the evidence), e-vincere is winning and driving out (evict), pro-vincia is territory that has been won (province). The name Vincent means \"winning\"."],
  ver: ["verus(真)来自印欧语 *weh₁-(真实、可信),同根的英语词是 warlock 里的 wær(誓约;warlock 是\"背誓者\"),德语 wahr(真)。罗马法官问证人 verum dicere(说真话),陪审团的 verdict 是 vere dictum(真话)。veritas 真理(哈佛校训),veri-ficare 使成真——核实(verify),veri-similis 像真的(逼真、可能),ad-verare 变成 aver(断言为真)。very 就是法语 verai(真的)——very good 本义\"真好\"。",
    "verus (true) comes from PIE *weh₁- (true, trustworthy), the root of the wær (oath) in warlock — an oath-breaker — and of German wahr. A Roman judge asked witnesses verum dicere, to speak the truth; a jury's verdict is vere dictum, the true word. veritas is truth (Harvard's motto), veri-ficare making true — verifying, veri-similis like the truth (plausible), ad-verare became aver, to assert as true. very is simply French verai, true — very good began as \"truly good\"."],
  ped: ["pes(脚,属格 pedis)来自印欧语 *pōd-,同根的英语词是 foot,希腊语 pous(pod-:octopus 八足,podium 是脚下的台)。罗马步兵是 pedites(用脚走的兵),pedica 是脚镣,ex-pedire 是\"把脚从绊索里解出来\"——脱身、准备好(expedite 加速,expedition 远征是\"轻装上路\"),im-pedire 是把脚绊住(impede 阻碍,impediment)。pedestrian 是走路的人,pedal 脚踏,pedigree 是法语 pied de grue(鹤脚)——家谱图上分叉的线像鹤的脚印。piedmont 是山脚,pawn(小卒)来自 pedo,步兵。",
    "pes (foot, genitive pedis) comes from PIE *pōd-, the root of English foot and Greek pous (pod-: octopus, podium — the base at your feet). Roman infantry were pedites, those who go on foot; a pedica was a foot-shackle; ex-pedire is freeing the foot from a snare — to get clear, to make ready (expedite, and an expedition travels light), im-pedire is snaring the foot (impede, impediment). A pedestrian goes on foot, a pedal is pushed by it, a pedigree is French pied de grue, crane's foot — the forking lines of a family tree looked like its print. Piedmont is the mountain's foot; a pawn comes from pedo, a foot-soldier."],
  mort: ["mors(死,属格 mortis)来自印欧语 *mer-(死、消失),同根有英语 murder、德语 Mord,希腊语 brotos(凡人,ambrosia 是\"不死之物\")。罗马人说 mortalis(会死的——凡人),im-mortalis 不死的(众神);mortuus 死了的;mortarium(研钵)与此无关。mortgage 是古法语\"死的抵押\"——付清或违约时抵押就\"死\"了;mortify 本义\"使死\",修士的苦行,后来指羞辱到想死;mortician 是 1895 年美国殡葬业者仿 physician 造的体面词。amortize 是让债务慢慢\"死\"掉。",
    "mors (death, genitive mortis) comes from PIE *mer- (to die, disappear), the root of English murder and German Mord, and of Greek brotos (a mortal; ambrosia is \"un-death\"). Romans said mortalis (subject to death — a mortal), im-mortalis (deathless — the gods), mortuus (dead). A mortgage is Old French for a \"dead pledge\" — it dies when paid off or forfeited; mortify meant to put to death, the monk's discipline, then to shame someone to death; mortician was coined in 1895 by American undertakers on the model of physician. To amortize is to let a debt die slowly."],
  cord: ["cor(心,属格 cordis)来自印欧语 *ḱerd-,同根有希腊语 kardia(cardio-)、英语 heart、德语 Herz。罗马人认为心是勇气和记忆的座位:re-cordari 是\"把东西再放回心里\"——记住(record 本义记忆,后指记录),con-cordia 是心在一起(和睦,concord),dis-cordia 心不合(discord),mis-eri-cordia 是心为苦难者所动(mercy 的拉丁形式),cor-datus 是有心的——聪明(西班牙语 cuerdo 明智)。courage 是法语 corage(心气),accord 是心与心合拍,cordial 是发自内心的。",
    "cor (heart, genitive cordis) comes from PIE *ḱerd-, the root of Greek kardia (cardio-), English heart and German Herz. Romans placed courage and memory in the heart: re-cordari is putting something back into the heart — to remember (a record was first a memory, then a written one), con-cordia is hearts together (concord), dis-cordia hearts apart, mis-eri-cordia a heart moved by suffering (Latin mercy), cor-datus \"with heart\" — wise (Spanish cuerdo). courage is French corage, heart-spirit; accord is hearts in tune; cordial is from the heart."],
  min: ["minor(较小)、minimus(最小)、minuere(减少)来自印欧语 *mei-(小、少),同根的希腊语是 meiōn(较少),英语 mince(切碎)经法语来。罗马人 minuere 是减少,di-minuere 削减(diminish),minutus 是切细的——minuta 是一小时的第一次\"细分\"(minute,分钟;秒是第二次细分 secunda minuta)。minister 是\"较小的人\"——仆人,与 magister(较大的人,主人)相对;基督教把牧师、后来把政府的\"大臣\"都叫 minister,本义还是服务者。minus 是减号,minute(细微的)、minor(未成年、次要)、minimum、minority(少数)都在这里。",
    "minor (smaller), minimus (smallest) and minuere (to lessen) come from PIE *mei- (small, few), shared with Greek meiōn (less); English mince came through French. Romans minuere to reduce, di-minuere to cut down (diminish); minutus is cut fine — a minuta was the first fine division of an hour (a minute; the second division was the secunda minuta, the second). A minister is \"the lesser man\", a servant, opposite of magister, the greater man, the master; Christianity applied it to clergy and later states to their ministers — servants still, in name. minus is the sign of taking away; minute, minor, minimum and minority all keep the smallness."],
  prim: ["primus(第一)是 prior(更前)的最高级,来自印欧语 *per-(在前),同根的英语词是 first、for、fore。罗马人把每月一号叫 kalendae,把黎明叫 prima lux(第一道光);primus pilus 是军团第一大队的首席百夫长。princeps(prim-ceps,\"占第一位的人\")是奥古斯都给自己选的低调头衔——\"首席公民\",后来变成 prince;primarius 首要的,prim-ordium 最初的开端,prima-vera 意大利语\"第一个春\",premier 是法语的\"第一\"(总理),prime 是最好的时段或数,primate 是\"第一等的动物\"(灵长类),primitive 是最初状态的。",
    "primus (first) is the superlative of prior (earlier), from PIE *per- (in front), the root of English first, for and fore. Romans called dawn prima lux, the first light; the primus pilus was the senior centurion of a legion's first cohort. princeps (prim-ceps, one who takes first place) was the modest title Augustus chose — first citizen — which became prince; primarius is chief, prim-ordium the first beginning, Italian prima-vera the first spring, French premier simply \"first\" (a prime minister), prime the best hour or an indivisible number, a primate a first-rank animal, primitive what is in its first state."],
  firm: ["firmus(牢固)来自印欧语 *dʰer-(支撑、握牢),同根的梵语 dharma(支撑世界的法则)和英语 farm——farm 本义是 firma,固定付的租金(中世纪拉丁语),后来指按固定租金租的地。罗马人说 firmare 使牢固:af-firmare 是说定、断言(affirm),con-firmare 是加固、证实(confirm;基督教的坚信礼),in-firmus 是不牢的——病弱(infirmary 病房)。firmament(苍穹)是《圣经》拉丁译本用 firmamentum 译希伯来语的\"穹顶\"——被造得牢固的天。公司 firm 是德语 Firma(商号签名)经意大利语 firma(签名,\"使牢固\"的一笔)。",
    "firmus (firm) comes from PIE *dʰer- (to hold, support), the root of Sanskrit dharma (the law that upholds the world) and, surprisingly, of English farm — a firma was a fixed rent in medieval Latin, then land held for a fixed rent. Romans firmare to make fast: af-firmare to declare firmly (affirm), con-firmare to strengthen, to prove (confirm; the Christian rite of confirmation), in-firmus not firm — sickly (an infirmary). The firmament is the Vulgate's firmamentum for the Hebrew \"vault\" — the sky made firm. A business firm is German Firma via Italian firma, a signature — the stroke that makes a deal firm."],
  re: ["re-(再、回)是拉丁语独有的前缀,不见于希腊语或日耳曼语,词源不明——可能与 retro(向后)同根。它有两个方向:向回(re-vertere 转回来,re-ducere 带回,re-spondere 回应)和再来一次(re-novare 更新,re-petere 再要一次——重复)。罗马人也用它表示\"对抗\":re-sistere 站住顶回去(resist),re-bellare 再打一仗(rebel),re-pugnare 反击。中世纪以后它成了欧洲最能造词的前缀:法语 re-、英语 re-,连日耳曼词都能加(re-make、re-set)。研究 research 是法语 re-cercher,\"再绕一圈找\"。",
    "re- (again, back) is a purely Latin prefix, unknown in Greek or Germanic, of uncertain origin — perhaps kin to retro (backward). It points two ways: back (re-vertere to turn back, re-ducere to lead back, re-spondere to answer) and once more (re-novare to renew, re-petere to ask again — repeat). Romans also used it for standing against: re-sistere to stand and push back (resist), re-bellare to fight again (rebel), re-pugnare to fight back. After the Middle Ages it became Europe's most productive prefix — French re-, English re-, even on Germanic words (re-make, re-set). Research is French re-cercher, to go round searching once more."],
  sult: ["salire(跳)来自印欧语 *sel-(跳),同根的希腊语是 hallomai(跳)。在复合词里 sal- 变 -sil-/-sult-:in-sultare 本义\"跳到人身上\"(insult 侮辱就是扑上去),re-sultare 是跳回来、反弹——结果 result 是行动\"弹回来\"的东西(这个意义 15 世纪才出现),ex-sultare 跳出来——欢跃(exult),as-saltus 是跳上去攻击(assault),con-sulere/consult 与此无关。salmon(鲑)是 salmo,\"跳鱼\";somersault 是法语 sobresault(跳过头顶);sauté 是法语\"跳\"——在锅里跳的菜;salient 是\"跳出来的\"——显著的。",
    "salire (to leap) comes from PIE *sel- (to jump), shared with Greek hallomai. In compounds sal- becomes -sil-/-sult-: in-sultare meant to leap upon someone (an insult is a pouncing), re-sultare to spring back, rebound — a result is what bounces back from an action (a sense that appeared only in the 15th century), ex-sultare to leap out — to exult, as-saltus a leap upon (assault). Salmon is salmo, the leaping fish; somersault is French sobresault, a leap over the head; sauté is French \"jumped\" — food that jumps in the pan; salient is what leaps out — prominent."],
  plic: ["plicare(折叠)来自印欧语 *plek-(编织、折),同根的希腊语是 plekein(编),英语 fold、德语 falten 是远亲,-fold(two-fold)对应拉丁语的 -plex(du-plex 双层,sim-plex 只折一次——简单,multi-plex 多层)。罗马人 ex-plicare 是把卷轴摊开——解释(explicit 是摊开说明的),im-plicare 是折进去——牵连、隐含(imply、implicit、implicate),com-plicare 折在一起——复杂(complicate),ap-plicare 折上去贴住(apply),re-plicare 折回来——回答(reply、replica),sup-plicare 是跪下把身子折起来——恳求(supplication)。ply、pliers、pliable、employ(im-plicare 经法语)都是折。",
    "plicare (to fold) comes from PIE *plek- (to plait, fold), shared with Greek plekein (to weave); English fold and German falten are distant kin, and -fold (two-fold) matches Latin -plex (du-plex double, sim-plex folded once — simple, multi-plex many-layered). Romans ex-plicare unrolled a scroll — to explain (explicit is unfolded plainly), im-plicare folded in — to entangle, to imply (implicit, implicate), com-plicare folded together (complicate), ap-plicare folded onto (apply), re-plicare folded back — to reply (replica), sup-plicare bent the body to the ground — to beg (supplication). Ply, pliers, pliable and employ (im-plicare through French) are all folding."],
  vac: ["vacare(空着、闲着)与 vanus(空虚)、vastus(荒芜)同根,印欧语 *h₁weh₂-(空)。罗马人的 vacatio 是\"免役\"——空出来不用服兵役或不用上班,vacation 本义就是空闲(度假是 19 世纪美国的用法,英国人说 holiday)。vacuus 空的(真空 vacuum 是 17 世纪科学家直接借的拉丁词),vacans 空缺的(vacant、vacancy),e-vacuare 排空(evacuate),vanitas 虚空——《传道书》\"虚空的虚空\"就是 vanitas vanitatum;vanish 是 e-vanescere(消散)经法语;devastate 是把地方弄荒;waste 是 vastus 经诺曼法语——荒地。",
    "vacare (to be empty, to be free) belongs with vanus (empty) and vastus (desolate), PIE *h₁weh₂- (empty). A Roman vacatio was an exemption — time emptied of military or civic duty, so a vacation was leisure (the holiday sense is 19th-century American; the British say holiday). vacuus is empty (vacuum was borrowed straight from Latin by 17th-century scientists), vacans unfilled (vacant, vacancy), e-vacuare to empty out (evacuate), vanitas emptiness — Ecclesiastes' vanitas vanitatum, vanity of vanities; vanish is e-vanescere through French, devastate lays a place waste, and waste itself is vastus in Norman French — wasteland."],
  magn: ["magnus(大)来自印欧语 *meǵ-(大),同根有希腊语 megas(mega-)、梵语 maha(摩诃,如 Mahatma 圣雄)、英语 much 与 mickle。比较级 maior(更大)给了 major、majority、mayor(市长是城里\"更大的人\")、majesty(magnitas 的堂亲);最高级 maximus 给了 maximum、maxim(格言是\"最大的命题\")。magister 是\"更大的人\"——师傅、主人(master、maestro、Mr.),magni-ficus 是\"做大事的\"(magnificent),magn-animus 大心——宽宏,Magna Carta 是\"大宪章\"。majuscule 大写字母,mayor 与 major 是同一个词的法语和拉丁语形状。",
    "magnus (great) comes from PIE *meǵ- (big), the root of Greek megas (mega-), Sanskrit maha (as in Mahatma, great soul), English much and mickle. The comparative maior (greater) gave major, majority, mayor (the town's greater man) and majesty; the superlative maximus gave maximum and maxim (a saying that is the greatest proposition). A magister is the greater man — master, maestro, Mr.; magni-ficus is one who does great things (magnificent), magn-animus great-hearted, the Magna Carta the Great Charter. Majuscule is a big letter, and mayor and major are one word in French and Latin dress."],
  cern: ["cernere(筛、分辨、决定)来自印欧语 *krei-(筛分),同根的希腊语是 krinein(判断,critic、crisis 是\"判断的关头\")、英语 riddle(筛子)。农夫用 cribrum(筛子)cernere 谷糠,罗马法官 cernere 是审断,继承人 cernere hereditatem 是\"决定接受遗产\"。分词 cretus/certus 给了 certain(已分辨清楚的)、certify、de-cernere 判定(decree 法令是判下来的)、dis-cernere 分辨(discern、discreet 有分辨力的)、se-cernere 分开——secret 是被分开藏起来的,secretary 本是保管机密的人,secretion 是分泌出来的;con-cernere 是筛到一起——涉及(concern)。ex-crementum 是筛出去的渣。",
    "cernere (to sift, distinguish, decide) comes from PIE *krei- (to sieve), shared with Greek krinein (to judge — a critic judges, a crisis is the moment of judgement) and English riddle (a sieve). A farmer cernere chaff through a cribrum, a Roman judge cernere a case, an heir cernere hereditatem — decided to accept the estate. The participle cretus/certus gave certain (sifted clear), certify, de-cernere to decide (a decree is what is decided), dis-cernere to tell apart (discern, discreet), se-cernere to set apart — a secret is what is sifted aside and hidden, a secretary the keeper of it, a secretion what is set apart by a gland; con-cernere sifts together — concerns."],
  flu: ["fluere(流)来自印欧语 *bʰleu-(涌、胀),同根的英语词是 blow(吹)和 bloom(绽放),希腊语 phlyein(冒泡)。罗马人 fluere 说河水,flumen 是河,fluctus 是波浪(fluctuate 起伏),fluvius 河流;af-fluere 流向——富裕 affluent 是财富流过来,in-fluere 流进——influence 本是占星术语,星辰的\"流入\"影响人的命运;influenza(流感)是意大利语,15 世纪人以为是星辰流入引起的瘟疫,flu 是它的缩写。con-fluere 汇流,super-fluus 溢出来的——多余,fluidus 流动的,fluens 流利的(fluent 是话像水一样流)。",
    "fluere (to flow) comes from PIE *bʰleu- (to swell, gush), the root of English blow and bloom and Greek phlyein (to bubble). Romans fluere of rivers: flumen is a river, fluctus a wave (fluctuate), fluvius a stream; af-fluere is flowing toward — the affluent have wealth flowing in, in-fluere flowing into — influence began as astrology, the stars' flowing-in that shaped a fate; influenza is Italian, a 15th-century epidemic blamed on the stars' influence, and flu is its clipping. con-fluere is flowing together, super-fluus what overflows — superfluous, fluidus flowing, fluens fluent — speech that runs like water."],
  temp: ["tempus(时间)可能来自印欧语 *temp-(拉伸)——时间是被拉开的一段,或来自 *tem-(切)——时间是切出的一段;罗马人自己把它和 templum(占卜官在天上\"切出\"的观测区,后指神庙)联系在一起。tempestas 是\"一段天气\",后来专指风暴(tempest);temperare 是\"按时、按比例调配\"——调和(temper 脾气本义体液的调配,temperature 温度是冷热的调配,temperate 节制);tem-porarius 一时的(temporary),contem-porarius 同时的,ex-tempore 当场即兴,tense(时态)是 tempus 经法语 temps。",
    "tempus (time) may come from PIE *temp- (to stretch) — time as a stretched span — or *tem- (to cut) — time as a cut section; Romans themselves linked it to templum, the patch of sky an augur \"cut out\" for observation, later a temple. tempestas was a spell of weather, then specifically a storm (tempest); temperare was to mix in due time and proportion — to temper (a temper was the blend of bodily humours, temperature the blend of hot and cold, temperate well-mixed); tem-porarius is for a time (temporary), contem-porarius of the same time, ex-tempore on the spot; tense in grammar is tempus in its French shape temps."],
  liber: ["liber(自由的)来自印欧语 *h₁leudʰ-(生长、人民),同根有德语 Leute(人民)、希腊语 eleutheros(自由的):\"自由人\"本是\"属于本族人民的人\",与买来的奴隶相对。罗马的 liberi(复数)竟是\"孩子\"——家里生而自由的成员;Liber 也是酒神的名字,解放人的神。libertas 自由,liber-are 释放(liberate、deliver 是 de-liberare 经法语,\"解开交出\"),liber-alis 是\"配得上自由人的\"——慷慨、博雅(liberal arts 是自由人该学的技艺,liberal 政治含义要到 19 世纪),liber-tinus 是获释奴,后来指放荡者。书 liber 是另一个词(树皮)。",
    "liber (free) comes from PIE *h₁leudʰ- (to grow; a people), shared with German Leute (people) and Greek eleutheros (free): a free man was one who belonged to the people, as against a slave who was bought. Roman liberi (plural) meant children — the freeborn members of a house; Liber was also the wine-god, the liberator. libertas is freedom, liber-are to set free (liberate; deliver is de-liberare through French, to loose and hand over), liber-alis worthy of a free man — generous, broad (the liberal arts were what a free man should learn; the political sense dates only from the 19th century), liber-tinus a freed slave, later a libertine. liber the book is a different word (bark)."],
  nov: ["novus(新)来自印欧语 *newo-,这是印欧语里传播最广的形容词之一:希腊语 neos(neo-)、梵语 nava、英语 new、德语 neu、俄语 novyj。罗马人称新出现的人为 homo novus——祖上没人当过执政官的\"新人\",西塞罗就是;novellus 是新长出的(novel 小说本是意大利语 novella,\"新鲜事\"),in-novare 翻新(innovate),re-novare 恢复如新(renovate),novitas 新奇(novelty),novicius 新买的奴隶、新入门者(novice)。nova 是天文学里突然\"新\"亮起的星;news 是英语自己的复数,但 nouvelles 的法语思路一样。",
    "novus (new) comes from PIE *newo-, one of the most widespread Indo-European adjectives: Greek neos (neo-), Sanskrit nava, English new, German neu, Russian novyj. Romans called a man with no consul among his ancestors a homo novus, a new man — Cicero was one; novellus is newly grown (a novel was Italian novella, a piece of news), in-novare makes new (innovate), re-novare makes new again (renovate), novitas newness (novelty), novicius a newly bought slave, a newcomer (novice). A nova is a star that suddenly appears new; news is English's own plural, but French nouvelles thought the same way."],
  ser: ["serere(连接、排列)来自印欧语 *ser-(串起来),同根的希腊语是 eirein(串)、hormos(项链)。罗马人 serere 是把东西一个接一个排成串:series 是一串,sermo 是\"串起来的话\"(sermon 讲道),de-serere 是解开串、抛弃(desert 遗弃,deserter 逃兵),in-serere 插进串里(insert),as-serere 是把人拉到自己身边——主张(assert 本义在法庭上\"把奴隶拉到自己一边\"宣布他自由或属于自己),ex-sertus 伸出来(exert 用力)。另一个 serere(播种)同形不同源(satus,给了 season、Saturn)。",
    "serere (to join, string together) comes from PIE *ser- (to line up), shared with Greek eirein (to string) and hormos (a necklace). Romans serere things one after another in a row: a series is a string, a sermo a string of words (sermon), de-serere is unstringing, abandoning (desert, deserter), in-serere slipping into the string (insert), as-serere pulling someone to one's side — to assert (originally, in court, laying hands on a slave to declare him free or one's own), ex-sertus thrust out (exert). The other serere, to sow, looks the same but is unrelated (its satus gave season and Saturn)."],
  pater: ["pater(父)是印欧语 *ph₂tḗr,横跨欧亚:梵语 pitar、希腊语 patēr、英语 father、德语 Vater。它不只是生父,更是\"家长\":pater familias 对全家有生杀之权。罗马元老叫 patres(父老们),他们的后代是 patricii(贵族);patronus 是保护人,门客 cliens 依附于他(patron、pattern 是同一个词——pattern 本义\"可效法的保护人\");patria 是\"父亲的土地\"——祖国(patriot),patri-monium 是父亲传下的财产(patrimony),Jupiter 是 Iu-piter,\"天父\";perpetrate 与此无关,但 pattern、patron、patriarch 都是父。",
    "pater (father) is Indo-European *ph₂tḗr, spanning Eurasia: Sanskrit pitar, Greek patēr, English father, German Vater. It meant not just the begetter but the head of the house: the pater familias held power of life and death. Rome's senators were the patres, the fathers, and their descendants the patricii (patricians); a patronus protected his clients (patron, and pattern is the same word — originally a protector to be imitated); patria is the father's land — the fatherland (patriot), patri-monium the father's estate (patrimony), Jupiter is Iu-piter, sky-father. Patron, pattern and patriarch all keep the father."],
  son: ["sonus(声音)来自印欧语 *swenh₂-(发声),同根的英语词是 swan(会\"唱\"的鸟)和古英语 swinsian(奏乐)。罗马人 sonare 是发声,personare 是\"声音穿过\"——persona 是演员的面具,声音从面具的嘴穿出来,后来指角色、人(person、personal 都是戴面具的人);con-sonans 一起响的——辅音(要和元音一起才能响),dis-sonans 不协和,re-sonare 回响(resonance),sonata 是意大利语\"奏出来的\"(与 cantata\"唱出来的\"相对),sonnet 是普罗旺斯语\"小歌\",unison 同声,sonorous 洪亮。",
    "sonus (sound) comes from PIE *swenh₂- (to sound), the root of English swan (the singing bird) and Old English swinsian (to make music). Romans sonare to sound; personare is sound passing through — a persona was an actor's mask through whose mouth the voice came, then a role, then a person (person and personal are masked speakers); con-sonans sounds together — a consonant needs a vowel to ring, dis-sonans clashes, re-sonare echoes (resonance), a sonata is Italian for \"sounded\" (as against a cantata, sung), a sonnet Provençal for a little song, unison one sound, sonorous full-sounding."],
  sist: ["sistere(使站立、停住)是 stare(站)的使役形式,同一个印欧语根 *steh₂-。罗马人 sistere 是让人\"站到法庭上\"(出庭),也是让军队停步。as-sistere 站在旁边(assist 协助;法语 assister 至今是\"在场\"),con-sistere 站在一起——由…组成(consist)、稳定(consistent),ex-sistere 站出来——存在(exist 本义\"显现出来\"),in-sistere 站在上面不走(insist 坚持),per-sistere 一直站着(persist),re-sistere 站住顶回去(resist),sub-sistere 站在底下——维持生存(subsist),de-sistere 停下(desist)。armistice 是武器\"站住\"——停战。",
    "sistere (to make stand, to halt) is the causative of stare (to stand), from the same PIE root *steh₂-. Romans sistere someone to appear in court, and sistere an army to halt it. as-sistere stands beside (assist; French assister still means to be present), con-sistere stands together — consists of, is consistent, ex-sistere stands out — exists (existence was first emergence into view), in-sistere stands on and stays (insist), per-sistere keeps standing (persist), re-sistere stands and pushes back (resist), sub-sistere stands underneath — subsists, de-sistere stops (desist). An armistice is arms standing still."],
  sci: ["scire(知道)本义可能是\"切开、分辨\"——印欧语 *skei-(切),同根的英语词是 shed(分开)、science 的远亲 shin(胫,切成条的骨)。罗马人说 scientia 是知(science 直到 17 世纪都泛指一切学问),con-scientia 是\"与自己共同知道\"——良心(conscience)和意识(conscious 分化于 17 世纪英语),ne-scius 不知道的(nescient),prae-scius 预知(prescient),omni-scientia 全知,sci-licet(scire licet,\"可以知道\")是\"即、当然\"。scintilla(火花)与此无关。",
    "scire (to know) probably began as \"to cut, to tell apart\" — PIE *skei- (to cut), the root of English shed (to separate) and, distantly, shin (the bone cut into strips). Romans called knowledge scientia (science meant any learning until the 17th century), con-scientia knowing together with oneself — conscience, and consciousness (the two split in 17th-century English), ne-scius not knowing (nescient), prae-scius knowing beforehand (prescient), omni-scientia all-knowing, sci-licet (scire licet, \"one may know\") meaning \"namely, of course\". scintilla, a spark, is unrelated."],
  mar: ["mare(海)来自印欧语 *mori-,同根有英语 mere(湖)、德语 Meer、俄语 more,凯尔特语 mor(Armorica 是\"临海之地\",Morgan 是\"海生\")。罗马人把地中海叫 mare nostrum(我们的海);marinus 是海的(marine、mariner),mari-timus 临海的(maritime),sub-marinus 海下的;mermaid 是英语 mere(海)+ maid;marsh(沼泽)是日耳曼语同根。cormorant 是 corvus marinus(海乌鸦),rosemary 是 ros marinus(海的露水)——因为它长在海边。Marina 作为名字是\"海的女子\"。",
    "mare (sea) comes from PIE *mori-, the root of English mere (a lake), German Meer, Russian more and Celtic mor (Armorica is the land by the sea, Morgan means sea-born). Romans called the Mediterranean mare nostrum, our sea; marinus is of the sea (marine, mariner), mari-timus by the sea (maritime), sub-marinus beneath it; mermaid is English mere + maid, and marsh is a Germanic cousin. A cormorant is corvus marinus, the sea-crow; rosemary is ros marinus, sea-dew, because it grows on the shore. Marina as a name means woman of the sea."],
  cur: ["cura(关心、照料)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明——可能来自 *kois-(操心)。罗马人把它用于一切\"操心\"的事:cura 是治疗(cure)、是忧虑,curator 是受托管理某事的官员(管水道的 curator aquarum),pro-curare 是替人照料——procurator 是代理人、行省财务官,后来法语磨成 proxy 和 procure。se-curus 是\"没有操心\"——安全(secure、sure 是 securus 经法语磨损),ac-curatus 是\"精心照料过的\"——准确,curiosus 是\"操心太多的\"——好奇,manicure 是照料手,sinecure 是\"不用操心\"的闲职,curate 是照料灵魂的助理牧师。",
    "cura (care, concern) is a purely Latin word of uncertain origin — perhaps *kois- (to trouble oneself). Romans applied it to every kind of care: cura is a cure and also a worry, a curator an official charged with something (the curator aquarum ran the aqueducts), pro-curare to take care on another's behalf — a procurator was an agent or provincial treasurer, worn by French into proxy and procure. se-curus is \"without care\" — secure, and sure is securus ground down by French; ac-curatus is done with care — accurate; curiosus is over-careful — curious; a manicure is care of the hand, a sinecure a post without care, a curate the priest who cares for souls."],
  volv: ["volvere(滚)来自印欧语 *wel-(转、卷),同根的英语词是 wallow(打滚)、walk(古义\"翻滚\")、wheel 的远亲 well(涌出),希腊语 helix(螺旋)。罗马的书是卷轴 volumen(volume 本义一卷,后来指一册,再指体积);re-volvere 滚回来——revolution 先是天体的周转(哥白尼 1543 年的书名),1688 年后才指政治剧变;e-volvere 把卷轴展开(evolve 演化是慢慢展开),in-volvere 卷进去(involve),de-volvere 滚下来(devolve),con-volvere 卷在一起(convolvulus 旋花),vault(拱顶)是 volvere 经法语的\"卷起来的顶\",voluble 是话滚滚而出。",
    "volvere (to roll) comes from PIE *wel- (to turn, wind), the root of English wallow, walk (once \"to roll\") and Greek helix. A Roman book was a volumen, a scroll (a volume was a roll, then a bound book, then bulk); re-volvere is rolling back — a revolution was first a heavenly body's circuit (Copernicus' title in 1543) and only after 1688 a political upheaval; e-volvere unrolls the scroll (to evolve is to unfold slowly), in-volvere rolls in (involve), de-volvere rolls down (devolve), con-volvere rolls together (convolvulus, the bindweed), a vault is a rolled-over ceiling in French, and the voluble roll out words."],
  fort: ["fortis(强壮、勇敢)可能来自印欧语 *bʰerǵʰ-(高、坚固),同根的英语词是 borough、德语 Burg(堡)。罗马人说 fortis 既指体力也指德性——勇气;fortitudo 坚忍(四枢德之一),fortis 的名词化 forte 是意大利语音乐术语\"强\"(与 piano\"弱\"相对,钢琴 pianoforte 是能强能弱的琴),fortalitia 是堡垒(fortress、fort),forti-ficare 加固(fortify),com-fortare 是\"使强壮\"——安慰 comfort 本义给人力量,effort 是法语 esforcer(用力)。fortuna(命运)与此无关。",
    "fortis (strong, brave) probably comes from PIE *bʰerǵʰ- (high, fortified), the root of English borough and German Burg. For Romans fortis meant bodily strength and moral strength alike — courage; fortitudo was one of the four cardinal virtues; forte in Italian music means loud (opposite of piano, soft — a pianoforte can do both); a fortalitia was a stronghold (fortress, fort), forti-ficare to strengthen (fortify), com-fortare to make strong — comfort originally gave strength, and effort is French esforcer, to put forth strength. fortuna, fortune, is unrelated."],
  stell: ["stella(星)来自印欧语 *h₂stḗr,同根有希腊语 astēr(aster、asteroid、astro-)、英语 star、德语 Stern。拉丁语 stella 是 *ster-la 的缩合,所以看不出 r。罗马人说 stellatus 满天星的,con-stellatio 是占星家说的\"星群\"(constellation,星座),inter-stellaris 星际的。希腊分支更丰富:astro-nomia 星的规律,astro-logia 星的学问(古代两者不分),dis-aster 是\"坏星\"——星象不利带来的灾难,asterisk 是小星号 *。stellar 是明星般的;意大利语 stella、法语 étoile、西班牙语 estrella 都是这一颗星。",
    "stella (star) comes from PIE *h₂stḗr, shared with Greek astēr (aster, asteroid, astro-), English star, German Stern. Latin stella is *ster-la contracted, which hides the r. Romans said stellatus, starry, con-stellatio the astrologers' group of stars (constellation), inter-stellaris between the stars. The Greek branch is richer: astro-nomia the law of the stars, astro-logia their lore (the two were one science in antiquity), dis-aster an ill star — the calamity a bad configuration brings, an asterisk a little star *. Italian stella, French étoile and Spanish estrella are all this one star."],
  graph: ["希腊语 graphein(刻、写、画)来自印欧语 *gerbʰ-(刻划),同根的英语词是 carve、德语 kerben(刻痕)。希腊人先在陶片和蜡板上\"刻\",所以写和画是一个词——graphē 既是文字也是图画。gramma 是刻出的一个字母(grammar 是字母的学问,glamour 是 grammar 在苏格兰的变体——识字的人被当成会魔法),pro-gramma 是公开写出来的告示(program),dia-gramma 划出的图,tele-gramma 远处传来的字。-graphy 是各种\"写\":geo-graphy 写大地,bio-graphy 写一生,photo-graphy 用光写。graphite(石墨)是能写的矿物,graffiti 是意大利语\"划上去的\"。",
    "Greek graphein (to scratch, write, draw) comes from PIE *gerbʰ- (to carve), the root of English carve and German kerben (to notch). Greeks first scratched into potsherds and wax, so writing and drawing were one word — graphē is both text and picture. A gramma is one carved letter (grammar is the study of letters; glamour is a Scots variant of grammar — the literate were suspected of magic), a pro-gramma a notice written up in public (program), a dia-gramma a drawn figure, a tele-gramma letters from afar. -graphy is every kind of writing: geo-graphy writes the earth, bio-graphy a life, photo-graphy writes with light. Graphite is the mineral that writes; graffiti is Italian for \"scratched on\"."],
  fat: ["fari(说)来自印欧语 *bʰeh₂-(说),同根有希腊语 phēmi(我说)、phōnē(声音,phone)、英语 ban(公开宣告,后指禁令)和 boon(祈求)。拉丁语的 fatum 是\"神说出的话\"——命运(fate、fatal),fama 是\"人们说的话\"——名声(fame、famous,defame 是说坏话),fabula 是讲的故事(fable),in-fans 是\"不会说话的\"——婴儿(infant;infantry 步兵是意大利语\"小伙子们\"),fatalis 命定的,fateri 承认——con-fiteri 完全承认(confess),pro-fiteri 公开宣称(profess;professor 是公开讲学的人),af-fabilis 好说话的(affable),in-effabilis 说不出的(ineffable),praefatio 前言(preface)。",
    "fari (to speak) comes from PIE *bʰeh₂- (to speak), the root of Greek phēmi (I say) and phōnē (voice, phone), of English ban (a public proclamation, later a prohibition) and boon (a request). Latin fatum is what the god has spoken — fate (fatal), fama what people say — fame (famous; to defame is to speak ill), fabula a tale told (fable), in-fans one who cannot speak — an infant (infantry is Italian for \"the lads\"), fateri to admit — con-fiteri to admit fully (confess), pro-fiteri to declare openly (profess; a professor teaches in public), af-fabilis easy to talk to (affable), in-effabilis beyond words (ineffable), praefatio what is said before (preface)."],
  ali: ["alius(另一个)与 alter(两者中的另一个)来自印欧语 *h₂el-(那边的、另一个),同根有希腊语 allos(allo-,allegory 是\"说别的\"),英语 else 和 other(*an-tero-)。罗马人称外邦人 alienus(属于别人的——alien;alienate 是让财产或感情变成别人的),alibi 是\"在别处\"(不在场证明),alias 是\"在别的时候\"——化名,alter ego 是\"另一个我\",alter-are 使变成另一样(alter 改变),alter-natus 一个隔一个(alternate),alter-catio 你一句我一句的争吵(altercation),adulterare 是\"掺入别的东西\"——掺假(adulterate;通奸 adultery 是同一个词,婚姻里掺了别人),altruism 是 19 世纪孔德用 altrui(他人)造的。",
    "alius (another) and alter (the other of two) come from PIE *h₂el- (beyond, other), shared with Greek allos (allo-; an allegory \"speaks otherwise\") and English else and other (*an-tero-). Romans called a foreigner alienus, belonging to another (alien; to alienate is to make property or affection another's), an alibi is \"elsewhere\", an alias \"at another time\" — another name, an alter ego another self, alter-are to make otherwise (alter), alter-natus every other one (alternate), alter-catio a quarrel of one word against another (altercation), adulterare to mix in something other — adulterate (adultery is the same word: a marriage with another mixed in); altruism was coined by Comte in the 19th century from altrui, others."],
  lav: ["lavare(洗)来自印欧语 *leuh₃-(洗),同根有希腊语 louein(洗)、英语 lye(碱液)和 lather(泡沫)。罗马人的公共浴场是 lavatrina/latrina(洗浴处——latrine 后来专指厕所),lavabo(我将洗)是弥撒里神父洗手时念的诗句,后来指洗手池;lotio(洗液,lotion),di-luere 用水冲开(dilute),ab-luere 洗去(ablution),al-luvium 是水冲来的泥沙(冲积层),de-luge 是 di-luvium(大洪水)经法语,lavender(薰衣草)可能因用来洗衣熏香得名,laundry 是 lavanderia(洗衣处)经法语磨损;lava(熔岩)是意大利语,原指冲下山的\"水流\"。",
    "lavare (to wash) comes from PIE *leuh₃- (to wash), shared with Greek louein, English lye and lather. Rome's washing-place was the lavatrina or latrina (a latrine later meant only the toilet); lavabo, \"I shall wash\", is the psalm verse the priest says while washing his hands at Mass, later the basin itself; lotio is a wash (lotion), di-luere to wash apart (dilute), ab-luere to wash off (ablution), al-luvium silt washed up, deluge is di-luvium, the great flood, through French, lavender may be the plant used to scent the wash, laundry is lavanderia worn down by French, and lava is Italian for a torrent rushing downhill."],
  vol: ["velle(愿意,词干 vol-)来自印欧语 *welh₁-(想要、选择),同根有英语 will、well(如愿的状态)、德语 wollen、Wahl(选择)。罗马人 voluntas 是意愿,voluntarius 是自愿的(volunteer,17 世纪指自愿从军的人),bene-volus 愿人好的(benevolent),male-volus 愿人坏的(malevolent),voluptas 是意愿得到满足——快感(voluptuous),nolle(不愿,ne-velle)给了法语 nolens volens(不管愿不愿)。志愿 volition 是经院哲学从 velle 造的名词。volatile 与此无关(volare 飞)。",
    "velle (to wish, stem vol-) comes from PIE *welh₁- (to want, choose), the root of English will and well (the state of things going as willed), German wollen and Wahl (choice). Roman voluntas is will, voluntarius willing (a volunteer, in the 17th century, enlisted of his own will), bene-volus wishing well (benevolent), male-volus wishing ill (malevolent), voluptas will satisfied — pleasure (voluptuous), and nolle (not to wish) survives in nolens volens, willy-nilly. Volition is a scholastic noun built on velle. volatile is unrelated (volare, to fly)."],
  log: ["希腊语 logos(话语、道理、计算)来自 legein(收集、说)——与拉丁语 legere 同根(*leǵ-,收集)。希腊哲学从赫拉克利特起就把 logos 当作宇宙的理性秩序;《约翰福音》开头\"太初有道\"的\"道\"就是 logos。-logia 是\"关于…的学问\"(bio-logy、geo-logy、theo-logy),-logos 是\"研究…的人\"。logic 是 logikē technē(讲道理的技艺),ana-logia 是比例、类比,dia-logos 是两人之间的话,mono-logos 一个人的话,pro-logos 前言,epi-logos 后语,cata-logos 一条条列下来的清单,apo-logia 辩护词(苏格拉底的申辩),logarithm 是纳皮尔 1614 年造的\"比数\"。",
    "Greek logos (word, reason, reckoning) comes from legein (to gather, to say) — cognate with Latin legere (*leǵ-, to collect). Greek philosophy from Heraclitus on made logos the rational order of the cosmos; the \"Word\" that opens John's Gospel is logos. -logia is the study of something (bio-logy, geo-logy, theo-logy), -logos the one who studies it. logic is logikē technē, the art of reasoning; ana-logia is proportion, analogy; a dia-logos words between two, a mono-logos one speaker's words, a pro-logos words before, an epi-logos words after, a cata-logos a list read down, an apo-logia a speech in defence (Socrates' Apology); logarithm was Napier's 1614 coinage, a \"ratio-number\"."],
  du: ["duo(二)是印欧语 *dwóh₁,几乎每种欧洲语言都有它:希腊语 dyo、英语 two、德语 zwei、俄语 dva、梵语 dva。拉丁语 duo 生出 dualis(成对的,dual)、duplex(折两层,duplex、duplicate)、dubius(\"两条路之间\"——犹豫、可疑,doubt 是 dubitare 经法语)、duellum(古 bellum 的写法,后被理解为\"两人之战\"——决斗)、duodecim(十二,dozen 是法语 douzaine)、bi-(bis,两次:bicycle、binary、biscuit 是\"烤两次\")。意大利语 duetto 是二重唱(duet),dozen、double(duplus)、diploma(希腊语,对折的文书)都是二。",
    "duo (two) is Indo-European *dwóh₁, present in nearly every European language: Greek dyo, English two, German zwei, Russian dva, Sanskrit dva. Latin duo bred dualis (in pairs, dual), duplex (folded twice — duplex, duplicate), dubius (between two ways — hesitant, doubtful; doubt is dubitare through French), duellum (an archaic spelling of bellum, war, later reread as a fight of two — a duel), duodecim (twelve; dozen is French douzaine), bi- (bis, twice: bicycle, binary, biscuit — baked twice). Italian duetto is a duet, and double (duplus) and diploma (Greek, a paper folded in two) are two as well."],
  grand: ["grandis(大、成熟、庄重)在古典拉丁语里比 magnus 少见,却在晚期口语里取而代之——所以法语 grand、意大利语 grande、西班牙语 grande 都用它而不用 magnus(magnus 只留在 Charlemagne\"大查理\"这类固定词里)。它可能来自印欧语 *gʷrendʰ-(胀大),同根的希腊语 brenthos(骄傲)。grand-father 是法语 grand-père 的英语仿造(\"大父亲\"),grandeur 宏伟,grandiose 浮夸,ag-grandize 扩大,grandiloquent 说大话的;西班牙贵族 grandee 是\"大人物\"。拉丁语自己的 grandis 还指\"年岁大的\"——grandaevus 高龄。",
    "grandis (big, full-grown, stately) was rarer than magnus in classical Latin but replaced it in late speech — which is why French grand, Italian grande and Spanish grande use it, magnus surviving only in fossils like Charlemagne, Charles the Great. It may come from PIE *gʷrendʰ- (to swell), shared with Greek brenthos (pride). Grandfather is English copying French grand-père, the \"great father\"; grandeur is greatness, grandiose inflated, ag-grandize to make greater, grandiloquent big-talking; a Spanish grandee is a great man. Latin grandis also meant advanced in years — grandaevus, aged."],
  tect: ["tegere(覆盖)来自印欧语 *(s)teg-(盖),同根有希腊语 stegos(屋顶)、英语 thatch(茅草顶)、deck(甲板,经荷兰语)、德语 Dach(屋顶)。分词 tectus 给了 tectum(屋顶)和 protect(pro-tegere,在前面盖住——保护)、detect(de-tegere,揭开盖子——发现)。toga(罗马公民的长袍)就是\"盖身的布\";tegula 是瓦片(tile 经古英语),in-tegumentum 外皮,architect 与此无关(希腊语 tektōn 木匠)。tile、thatch、toga、protect——从屋顶到长袍到保护,都是一个\"盖\"。",
    "tegere (to cover) comes from PIE *(s)teg- (to cover), shared with Greek stegos (roof), English thatch and deck (via Dutch), German Dach (roof). The participle tectus gave tectum (a roof), protect (pro-tegere, to cover in front) and detect (de-tegere, to take the cover off — to uncover). The toga, the Roman citizen's robe, is simply the cloth that covers; a tegula is a roof-tile (tile, through Old English), an in-tegumentum an outer skin; architect is unrelated (Greek tektōn, a builder). Tile, thatch, toga, protect — from roof to robe to shelter, one act of covering."],
  ergo: ["希腊语 ergon(工作)来自印欧语 *werǵ-(做、工作),同根的英语词是 work、德语 Werk,拉丁语没有直接的对应。希腊人的 energeia 是\"在工作中的状态\"——亚里士多德用它指\"实现\"(energy 作物理量是 1807 年托马斯·杨定的);-urgia 是各种\"做工\":metall-urgy 炼金属,chir-urgia 是\"手的工作\"——外科(surgery 是它经法语磨损),lit-urgy 是\"公众的工作\"——雅典富人出资办的公共事务,后指礼拜仪式,dramat-urgy 编剧,thaumat-urgy 行奇迹。organon(工具)是同根:organ 器官是身体的\"工具\",风琴 organ 是乐器工具,organize 是把东西做成有用的整体。allergy 是 1906 年造的\"异样反应\"。",
    "Greek ergon (work) comes from PIE *werǵ- (to do, work), the root of English work and German Werk; Latin has no direct cognate. Greek energeia was the state of being at work — Aristotle's term for actuality (energy as a physical quantity was fixed by Thomas Young in 1807); -urgia is a kind of working: metall-urgy works metal, chir-urgia is hand-work — surgery, its French-worn form, lit-urgy was public work — civic duties paid for by rich Athenians, later divine service, dramat-urgy play-making, thaumat-urgy wonder-working. organon (a tool) is the same root: an organ is the body's tool, the church organ a musical tool, to organize is to make things into a working whole. Allergy was coined in 1906 as \"other-working\", an altered reaction."],
  lumin: ["lux(光,属格 lucis)与 lumen(光源、灯)来自印欧语 *leuk-(亮),同根有希腊语 leukos(白,leukaemia 白血病)、英语 light、德语 Licht、梵语 ruc(闪耀)。罗马人的 lucerna 是油灯,Lucifer 是\"带光者\"(晨星金星,后来才成魔鬼的名字),lucidus 明亮的(lucid 清晰),e-lucidare 使明白(elucidate),il-lustris 被照亮的——显赫(illustrious),il-lustrare 照亮、配图(illustrate),lumen 给了 luminous、illuminate(点亮;中世纪手抄本的彩绘就叫 illumination)、luminary(发光体——明星),luna(月亮,lucna)是\"发光的那个\",lunatic 是月亮引发的疯狂。",
    "lux (light, genitive lucis) and lumen (a light, a lamp) come from PIE *leuk- (bright), shared with Greek leukos (white; leukaemia), English light, German Licht, Sanskrit ruc (to shine). A Roman lucerna was an oil lamp; Lucifer, the light-bringer, was the morning star Venus long before it named the devil; lucidus is bright (lucid), e-lucidare to make clear (elucidate), il-lustris lit up — distinguished (illustrious), il-lustrare to light up, to picture (illustrate); lumen gave luminous, illuminate (medieval manuscript painting was illumination) and luminary (a light-giver — a star), and luna (lucna), the moon, is \"the shining one\" — a lunatic was moon-struck."],
  dom: ["domus(房子)来自印欧语 *dem-(建造、房子),同根有希腊语 domos、英语 timber(建房的木料)、德语 Zimmer(房间,古义木造建筑)。dominus 是\"房子的主人\",阴性 domina(dame、madame、donna、Doña 的来源);dominari 做主人——统治(dominate),dominium 所有权(domain、dominion),domesticus 家里的(domestic),domicilium 住所。中世纪把 dominus 用来称主(Dominus,主日 dies dominica——意大利语 domenica、西班牙语 domingo、法语 dimanche 都是\"主日\"),Dom 是葡萄牙、意大利的敬称,Don 是西班牙的,duomo(意大利语主教座堂)是 domus Dei(神的家)。danger 竟也在此:古法语 dangier 是 dominiarium(主人的权力),落在人家权力之下就是危险。",
    "domus (house) comes from PIE *dem- (to build; house), shared with Greek domos, English timber (house-wood) and German Zimmer (a room; originally a timbered building). dominus is the master of the house, feminine domina (dame, madame, donna, Doña); dominari is to be master — to dominate, dominium ownership (domain, dominion), domesticus of the house, domicilium a dwelling. The Middle Ages made dominus the Lord: dies dominica, the Lord's day, is Italian domenica, Spanish domingo, French dimanche; Dom is a Portuguese and Italian honorific, Don a Spanish one, and an Italian duomo is domus Dei, God's house. Even danger belongs here: Old French dangier was dominiarium, a lord's power — to be in someone's power was to be in danger."],
  equ: ["aequus(平、等)是拉丁语独有的词,词源不明。罗马测量员用 aequare 把地整平,法学家把 aequitas(公平)与 ius(严格的法)相对——衡平法 equity 至今是英美法的一支。aequator 是把天球平分的圈(赤道),aequi-noctium 昼夜等长(equinox),aequi-librium 天平两边平衡(equilibrium),aequi-valens 等值(equivalent),ad-aequare 使相等(adequate 是\"够得上的\"),aequ-animitas 心平(equanimity),in-iquus 不平——不义(iniquity),equal、equation、equator 都是同一个\"平\"。马 equus 与此无关(印欧语 *h₁eḱwos,equestrian)。",
    "aequus (level, equal) is a purely Latin word of unknown origin. Roman surveyors aequare to level ground; jurists set aequitas (fairness) against ius (strict law) — equity is still a branch of English law. The aequator is the circle that halves the sky (equator), aequi-noctium equal night (equinox), aequi-librium the balance of two pans (equilibrium), aequi-valens equal in worth (equivalent), ad-aequare to make equal to (adequate is what comes up to the mark), aequ-animitas an even mind (equanimity), in-iquus uneven — unjust (iniquity); equal, equation and equator are all one levelness. equus, the horse, is unrelated (PIE *h₁eḱwos; equestrian)."],
  dur: ["durus(硬)来自印欧语 *deru-(树、坚固),同根有英语 tree、true(像树一样可靠)、trust、德语 treu(忠诚)、希腊语 drys(橡树)、梵语 dāru(木)。硬的东西才持久:durare 是变硬、也是持续(during 是 durant,\"在持续之中\";endure 是 in-durare 硬撑),duratio 持续时间(duration),durabilis 耐用(durable),ob-durare 心硬到底(obdurate),in-duratus 硬化。dour(阴沉)是苏格兰人的 durus,duress 是 duritia(严酷)——在胁迫之下。硬——持久——可靠——真:从 durus 到 true 是同一棵树。",
    "durus (hard) comes from PIE *deru- (tree, firm), the root of English tree, true (as reliable as a tree), trust, German treu (loyal), Greek drys (oak), Sanskrit dāru (wood). What is hard lasts: durare is to harden and to last (during is durant, \"while it lasts\"; to endure is in-durare, to harden and bear), duratio how long a thing lasts (duration), durabilis long-lasting (durable), ob-durare to stay hard-hearted (obdurate), in-duratus hardened. Dour is durus in Scots, duress is duritia — harshness, being under it. Hard — lasting — reliable — true: from durus to true is the same tree."],
  merc: ["merx(商品,属格 mercis)是拉丁语借自伊特鲁里亚语的词,词源不明——罗马人的商业词汇不少来自这个更早的城市文明。Mercurius(墨丘利)是商人和小偷的神,他的日子 dies Mercurii 是法语 mercredi、西班牙语 miércoles、意大利语 mercoledì(星期三)。mercatus 是市场(market、mercato、mercado),mercator 商人(墨卡托是荷兰地图学家的拉丁化名),merces 是货款、工钱——mercy 竟从这里来:晚期拉丁语 merces 指\"报答\",教会用它说上帝给人的恩赏,法语 merci(谢谢)和英语 mercy(怜悯)同出一源。mercenary 是拿钱打仗的,merchandise 是法语的货物,commerce 是一起做买卖。",
    "merx (goods, genitive mercis) is Latin borrowed from Etruscan, origin unknown — much of Rome's trade vocabulary came from that older urban civilisation. Mercurius, Mercury, was the god of merchants and thieves, and his day, dies Mercurii, is French mercredi, Spanish miércoles, Italian mercoledì (Wednesday). mercatus is a market (mercato, mercado), mercator a merchant (Mercator was a Flemish map-maker's Latin name), merces the price or wages — and mercy comes from here: late Latin merces meant a reward, the Church used it for God's gracious reward, and French merci (thank you) and English mercy share the source. A mercenary fights for pay, merchandise is French goods, commerce trading together."],
  sol: ["sol(太阳)来自印欧语 *seh₂wel-,同根有希腊语 hēlios(helio-)、英语 sun、德语 Sonne、梵语 sūrya——同一个根在各语言里换了不同的后缀。罗马人的 dies Solis(太阳日)是英语 Sunday、德语 Sonntag;基督教把它改叫 dies dominica(主日),所以罗曼语的星期日不再是太阳。solaris 太阳的,sol-stitium 是太阳\"站住\"的日子(至日),in-solatio 日晒,parasol 是意大利语\"挡太阳\"(para- 是 parare,挡),solarium 是罗马屋顶的晒台。Sol Invictus(不败的太阳)是 3 世纪罗马的国神,其生日 12 月 25 日后来给了圣诞。",
    "sol (sun) comes from PIE *seh₂wel-, shared with Greek hēlios (helio-), English sun, German Sonne, Sanskrit sūrya — one root wearing different endings in each language. The Roman dies Solis, sun's day, is English Sunday and German Sonntag; Christianity renamed it dies dominica, the Lord's day, so in the Romance languages Sunday lost the sun. solaris is solar, sol-stitium the day the sun stands still (solstice), in-solatio sunning, a parasol Italian for \"ward off the sun\" (para- from parare, to ward off), a solarium the sun-terrace on a Roman roof. Sol Invictus, the Unconquered Sun, was Rome's state god in the 3rd century, and his birthday, 25 December, later became Christmas."],
});
